[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e335f1ce-f85b-55fb-9898-0984e81e5a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397959} {:answer/id #uuid "058877bc-b659-5e3e-87cf-faeec5fcf705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397960} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8caafa-3ec3-522c-ad87-8c146d142f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397961} {:answer/id #uuid "354ff6f5-50a5-5ef5-9619-794c2e23d89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397962} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e0a436-d109-5391-b4da-ce44a96c863e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc7b100-8716-5ef0-ac33-e0d012d77654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8259cc12-8425-5d9e-923a-3106edaae5d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109943" :question/problem "$${∬}_{D}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}3-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+4u+5v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+4u+5v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+4u+5v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}3\\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5545b0b7-751d-5798-9bd4-dfa8faf23f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397966} {:answer/id #uuid "a470c598-2c0a-5ceb-ac0e-1fb73e235141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{9{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397967} {:answer/id #uuid "dade9321-241a-5817-902c-2195520cffda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e02df-d389-5236-a15d-ad10a54c5136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397969} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a959ef-e722-5be5-9bcf-f1870eca1b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397969} :answer-field/id #uuid "96083256-eb07-5ebd-a86e-e5865f463363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c235bda4-b647-59f5-b137-7fc5bc86576a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109947" :question/problem "$${∬}_{D}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}6u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 6u & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}6u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6u \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6u)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{u}^{2}+4+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72{u}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(18{u}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a16278f-a652-5ce3-b9f8-9f80d8b88232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397973} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1a6ff8-553d-5774-99ac-38531f54fba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397974} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d50f4f-f2ad-5974-83b1-c8764b04094b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397975} {:answer/id #uuid "c4340729-f530-5302-81b0-b5b30177bfba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397976} {:answer/id #uuid "21efbbd9-845c-5789-8305-5927df10c402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b095c98-f0c4-52f0-b54c-c5176a3e6c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0aca92da-c648-5c8b-ae46-3b81f8b99c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-109951" :question/problem "$${∬}_{D}\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 4.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(-2{u}^{2})^{2}+(4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326be129-c932-54e7-bd6e-d61f8a6c1750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398104} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f6915c-a30b-538c-be4c-c5d4feafc456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398105} {:answer/id #uuid "d929af17-b6b6-5bb1-9948-38e8c5714648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398106} {:answer/id #uuid "e257789f-7b07-5075-adc9-57e60483dc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398107} {:answer/id #uuid "65143245-b12a-5ed5-a1a3-dc55de2b261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}(2-5x)^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398105} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d582062-813e-5b4d-b116-6b4cb9d5d390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "201b1303-edfe-5a94-b70a-3687fd03d284" :question/math-academy-id "q-113502" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 18(2-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18(2-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 18{u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[-\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}+C\\right] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{3}{5}(2-5x)^{6}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(2-5x)^{5}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =18(2-5x)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bb5e34-8a7e-5077-9056-44171c31d68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398111} {:answer/id #uuid "73df82c1-76f6-50ea-99b9-53a33ba7607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398112} {:answer/id #uuid "cfab3df7-4bb6-5eeb-9342-3841991827b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398113} {:answer/id #uuid "1e49762e-1e14-5248-8c9e-16a5e673bda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398114} {:answer/id #uuid "15a56691-9975-5ae2-aa7b-fda010fe63b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398115} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13dea0f-f218-5cd2-a503-c7b2250d51fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1948aa2d-5fbb-59f5-8ccc-6dd9cfd6ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-113509" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-2)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (x-2)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-2)+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f0d601-5b9e-5b34-b1e4-35332ff61132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20(9-5x)^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398118} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0f063f-eba9-55db-a617-104bbe47e493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398119} {:answer/id #uuid "199a03cb-71ce-557c-b3af-135bd3564f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398120} {:answer/id #uuid "418bd3f4-e4c0-5bc7-b4ac-5541696af9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398121} {:answer/id #uuid "51978e0d-c725-5111-8fd8-58bfe89fa4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2(9-5x)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398121} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b49b60e-8217-5844-bea9-fe48c12aaf25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d34fadec-e1db-5f0e-b3fb-64f3326f1376" :question/math-academy-id "q-113517" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{(9-5x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=9-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(9-5x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-2)(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(9-5x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(9-5x)}^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "77a1d4df-21dd-55c4-aa5e-6b99c9ef011e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398134} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb579ad-3bd5-5860-ac4c-55d8de9d5206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{4},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f31a39b5-ddea-5435-ad94-3285f142b3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398223} {:answer/id #uuid "d779dff4-110a-560a-8225-d7034da334e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=0,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398224} {:answer/id #uuid "7062ec22-777c-5ee0-8265-a643d026997a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398225} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9e9422-5b2e-544f-8018-890315cb6fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398226} {:answer/id #uuid "2e82f7e5-3527-59eb-986a-5f3b990235ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398226} :answer-field/id #uuid "379f8f68-62eb-5f9b-956e-558338f11c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "942f342e-d375-5134-864c-b37213f6cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-121736" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=\\left\\langle \\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle ,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left({t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2t-4}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \\left\\langle \\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)\\right\\rangle  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=2, \\\\ 4{t}^{2}(t-2)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5deb70e-b37a-53c8-afc0-f67c23624ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{5t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398244} {:answer/id #uuid "173b53e6-f27b-52cd-8b04-8ca55a358b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398245} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e7a6e3-452b-57d7-bee7-a456f2cb7f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398246} {:answer/id #uuid "84a43112-1761-5fc6-adc6-94c0086bfedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}+5,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398247} {:answer/id #uuid "a45de57b-7567-5153-b536-079a7493dfff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398248} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f3589e3-9365-51aa-8bbb-05d8e6a481a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "811bbc59-9fc3-5b4b-bd9e-2e09752150a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123292" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩$, then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{2t}+5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)+{c}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."} {:answer/id #uuid "d5deb70e-b37a-53c8-afc0-f67c23624ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{5t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398244} {:answer/id #uuid "173b53e6-f27b-52cd-8b04-8ca55a358b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398245} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e7a6e3-452b-57d7-bee7-a456f2cb7f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398246} {:answer/id #uuid "84a43112-1761-5fc6-adc6-94c0086bfedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}+5,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398247} {:answer/id #uuid "a45de57b-7567-5153-b536-079a7493dfff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9e81bae-2705-548d-95b6-765f54a06b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{2}-4t,\\,\\frac{t}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398251} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a9a238-62ac-5621-ae23-bfa91834e092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle t+4,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398252} {:answer/id #uuid "fde38542-a315-509d-a3f3-05581da43b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398253} {:answer/id #uuid "773252d0-6e9f-5e6f-ad08-105990cdd9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle 2{t}^{4}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398254} {:answer/id #uuid "94d631bd-6a8a-5a5f-aee4-69b8325289a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398253} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e7c65b-0145-5085-9ab2-4dece927cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbfe4f27-7d39-5346-b6c3-d28ca1cafdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-123294" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle 4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}\\right\\rangle ,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\left\\langle 4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left\\langle 4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t & =\\left\\langle \\int (4{t}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{t}{4}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}+{c}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e81bae-2705-548d-95b6-765f54a06b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{2}-4t,\\,\\frac{t}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398251} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a9a238-62ac-5621-ae23-bfa91834e092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle t+4,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398252} {:answer/id #uuid "fde38542-a315-509d-a3f3-05581da43b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398253} {:answer/id #uuid "773252d0-6e9f-5e6f-ad08-105990cdd9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle 2{t}^{4}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{8}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398254} {:answer/id #uuid "94d631bd-6a8a-5a5f-aee4-69b8325289a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle {t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084dd163-8456-51dc-abdd-4a5eea95cc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398307} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a7ad62-1f98-5677-b05c-070f0505e36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398308} {:answer/id #uuid "8f677f3f-e28b-556f-866e-a1bb72653998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398309} {:answer/id #uuid "4327286d-a7cb-5d9d-8513-bee55df9202d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398310} {:answer/id #uuid "5860855f-9a3e-5177-8f82-b305b25602d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e390087c-f3c4-5e5d-895a-6dad0ea160b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9ec2fe9-559e-547b-851b-6351eb71769b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127333" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\right)⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{1/2}}⟨0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-4t⟨1,\\,2t⟩+(4{t}^{2}+1)⟨0,\\,2⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4t,\\,2\\,⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(4(1)^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4(1),\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}}⟨-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-2,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1)\\cdot T(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,\\,2⟩\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-2)(1)+(1)(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb3a5249-e2a5-57f9-8e7b-da061704300d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\sqrt{3},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398314} {:answer/id #uuid "60fb6e8c-68a1-5734-b535-b2fcb695213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398315} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9faa04-0ff3-58c0-9180-ad10d99b2911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398316} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ef47a-49f6-5f7c-85d7-c6f8163f2fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398317} {:answer/id #uuid "caeb96ab-86e7-5128-afdc-6b279a672893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61eec78-2599-57fe-a41d-57c6c6d95dc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d020d9fa-ec2e-5a71-a105-bba07f29c4ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-127583" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{2}{4+t}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{4+t}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{4+t}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{4+t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{(4+t)}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{4+t}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2}{{(4+0)}^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{2}{4+0}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩+4⟨1,0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}⟨3,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}⟨3,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f58a28b-8a69-51ea-9356-d11140bffa6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398321} {:answer/id #uuid "94b7fac8-b0d0-5073-bd6a-e851bd44f67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398322} {:answer/id #uuid "25f226b0-16a9-5f5d-aa1f-dd6c1a36838c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398323} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f5b218-1e89-5311-a291-97235fe4d002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398324} {:answer/id #uuid "413846d8-f5f3-5bd5-b545-4953038ddd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398323} :answer-field/id #uuid "d103e9f6-a851-56bd-8b50-38d9ebf224a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "811d16c6-f719-59c2-bae0-963973f1dd90" :question/math-academy-id "q-127598" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{0}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}q(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+2\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0\\right\\rangle \\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1(1)+\\sqrt{2}(0)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1+0-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61b2cbe-e283-5d14-b24c-72dded057638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\,j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398328} {:answer/id #uuid "843c6fdf-9f38-56ac-8525-881bcff219b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398329} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b83354-a308-50ea-9ee4-85aaf20726b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1c6878-8ab0-51e2-8005-62b32908bc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7cd7b0-03c7-513f-a03d-32eb59e6232b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a28c00-4228-59a1-bd55-b03951c2d0fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-127600" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e9ceb5-4080-500e-8bcd-622b7ae80448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398440} {:answer/id #uuid "ce317114-fb9b-5281-ada3-55ec1a4af574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398441} {:answer/id #uuid "40f9a0c5-f923-59d8-b738-65afcd30e84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398442} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf9e771-6593-534a-bec0-24bdaeea40ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398443} {:answer/id #uuid "5a488685-74ca-5b6f-8ca3-f7e24eb44fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398441} :answer-field/id #uuid "679ffd13-8aa6-5264-8220-82a24974cff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32164094-a2a0-521c-8478-fb3407c48f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140579" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a71e2e5e4cd2fddac779a7c2d21d072663f406bc6595ef9c7e15c885bbb83889.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=5$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2522b823-b6ab-5931-a981-d1897549cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398502} {:answer/id #uuid "b6492003-1c71-5f35-931d-cbf5fe819f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398503} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c90f3c-320e-52bd-b987-c216a3f4625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398504} {:answer/id #uuid "ee730d87-2f06-5106-8cf9-62838e1fb416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398505} {:answer/id #uuid "74974743-e247-56a3-b446-988e3a2779de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398506} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f44abc-ac48-5c5f-840d-7b78fa1a5463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e82a0cbf-06e5-5031-a4c7-160ebc24fbd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161086" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=2x,\\,y=5x,\\,y=\\frac{3}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\,,\\,\\,\\sqrt{uv}),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=2x,\\,y=5x,\\,y=\\frac{3}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5,\\,xy=3,\\,xy=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to \\left(\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\,,\\,xy\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=xy.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=u\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to \\left(\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\sqrt{uv}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82fdd23c-cc42-585e-88f4-5b182b40e621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398516} {:answer/id #uuid "23aa505d-ac0a-5049-b866-d1c5cc38bc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398517} {:answer/id #uuid "e4493f5f-d9a0-53f7-a4c1-2310218fdc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398518} {:answer/id #uuid "8e689bac-8fa3-596f-82fa-c4985e2215d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398519} {:answer/id #uuid "8b17b653-bdb9-591a-a066-41676556f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5u-6v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398520} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15555d0-ef8f-523d-8686-3e2c75b76cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eec4b66c-4af6-5b13-942b-337cb7b8732a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161357" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-2)+(2u-v)\\cdot (2)+(3u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u-2v+4u-2v+3u-2v \\\\  & =5u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-6v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({\\color{blue}5u-6v})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c22e69-9068-5d72-8593-ce78861036f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398523} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4db3f6-9c97-526b-8cb2-065c8f5de466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398524} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2e8a80-65c9-5d70-9b0e-0e801e7e718d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(u+v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398525} {:answer/id #uuid "9f182d4d-0129-52ff-90b2-56e5acb487b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(u-v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398526} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e4fd3a-37bb-5bcd-85c6-185c9a1d61c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+uv-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398523} :answer-field/id #uuid "396da9a4-de3b-5bd7-8b5e-dfc780e10bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5e1155b-c484-5f31-a815-946d2d63d6df" :question/math-academy-id "q-161359" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨zy,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨zy,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)v,\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)v,\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)v\\cdot (-1)+(u-v)u\\cdot (1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-uv+{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}-uv+uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨zy,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26585b8c-e264-5ec5-9955-637ca3eab6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(14,30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398606} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffd5745-f4c8-5a19-adc0-b37920b54995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\frac{11}{2},12\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398607} {:answer/id #uuid "5225db26-fbee-5e92-ab23-1c2785ab7665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{11}{2},12\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398608} {:answer/id #uuid "de5685b5-e86e-5a26-8d7a-36145d147fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398609} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4aa85c-9ff1-574b-86fb-ac6e431fa3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7805dc28-4db0-584d-a7f0-aae461b099c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29b73a1c-11c1-5790-9fa9-ce94ade43d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-165039" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+4t,3{t}^{2}+9t\\right\\rangle ,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+4t\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+9t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =⟨c,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3t+4 &=c \\\\ 6t+9 &=3c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=3t+4.$ Substituting $c=3t+4$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+9 & =3(3t+4) \\\\ 6t+9 & =9t+12 \\\\ 3t & =-3 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}+4(-1),3(-1)^{2}+9(-1)\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle -\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $\\left(-\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffd5745-f4c8-5a19-adc0-b37920b54995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\frac{11}{2},12\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398607} {:answer/id #uuid "5225db26-fbee-5e92-ab23-1c2785ab7665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{11}{2},12\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398608} {:answer/id #uuid "de5685b5-e86e-5a26-8d7a-36145d147fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398609} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4aa85c-9ff1-574b-86fb-ac6e431fa3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{5}{2},-6\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "857f6143-ac04-5d22-89ea-beac8d59b8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398796} {:answer/id #uuid "f5db497d-954e-5901-8ce1-2edb8dbfd8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398797} {:answer/id #uuid "8f60e247-41c2-5156-8b2e-8747eea0ba83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398798} {:answer/id #uuid "e41a712c-2ed2-5a81-a670-133fc56da2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398799} {:answer/id #uuid "fc732604-2155-534f-9edc-e5130ea51f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398796} :answer-field/id #uuid "13472b1e-4579-5a57-a0b2-2766d478d884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9de74d3-6110-5563-9c72-d29ba30c9f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-353420" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+1,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x+1 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =3{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =3{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =\\left(0+1,\\,0+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(1,\\frac{5}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(1+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+5+5+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $4$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $4$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff1c66d-1d33-56da-9000-6a48f77d70b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398803} {:answer/id #uuid "59a9d660-5d72-59b1-8d57-cd43b45837ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398804} {:answer/id #uuid "72270a82-402c-5b1c-a7fa-efadd9b92e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398805} {:answer/id #uuid "e27eca17-dbb0-5a83-9df3-48590e996ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398806} {:answer/id #uuid "6b061bdd-e433-5e95-a68c-114cb85724ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398805} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e73ca18-156e-5b8f-a2fb-86a3129cdd41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cea7789b-749c-5c46-8b2f-4e0087e42426" :question/math-academy-id "q-353421" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+3,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with one step to approximate $y(0).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},0\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+1,\\,-1+2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+4+4+3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54bbe447-6b4b-5f58-83a2-faffcfe8e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398864} {:answer/id #uuid "80391d2b-cd87-5377-b8af-b4ce9fbda203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398865} {:answer/id #uuid "2f831889-41ef-5e2e-b738-5ac690d2e875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398866} {:answer/id #uuid "5385b713-f0ec-57f9-9bc4-e473da877f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398867} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e7d0c1-250d-5300-a991-d81aee243a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398866} :answer-field/id #uuid "95508699-f6d9-5815-8c2e-e441b5ff17ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7fa8c48-a61b-5fbf-8ae6-af7bb5265a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49027" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\cdot (1+x)-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)-2x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}(1+x)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{2}}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-2}{2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{18\\sqrt{2}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739b7d37-465f-53a0-92fd-a46f218e891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398871} {:answer/id #uuid "c966cc5b-2f5d-56fa-94a2-562519ed3dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398872} {:answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} {:answer/id #uuid "bede226b-b6b5-5601-a51f-99f2392a59d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398874} {:answer/id #uuid "c91eab72-8ce7-50be-b8c3-3258720d8056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f41607-89e1-5a62-888e-441db9b42ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78d0396e-82eb-542b-a43f-b6df87f217ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49431" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(1-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}\\left[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\right] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{1-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(1-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(1-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(1-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70657675-2db0-5a10-9cf9-052f05edf016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} {:answer/id #uuid "3909aff4-3810-5b83-850f-f988be7ba4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398879} {:answer/id #uuid "4061afb3-02dc-5f4e-ae19-437ea14959c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398880} {:answer/id #uuid "3295113f-e7ef-59d3-92a7-66e977a1b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398881} {:answer/id #uuid "732c775a-c5f4-5d4e-91c3-0fdfab8cfb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad56171-faf0-523d-b089-53ed18731d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61d8dd43-99c6-5162-9111-9fc96bb88dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49439" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=x-100.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{11/4}}{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}(x-100)^{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-100)^{11/4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{4}(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-100)+0 \\\\  & =(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f1bb19-f6a2-538a-8094-300b1e55cc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398885} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8f42a5-cc05-5072-93b3-0b55ad819b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398886} {:answer/id #uuid "1c531862-1246-5d33-83db-faf2c5f53bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398887} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef3e88-28df-595f-88f1-2280a46131c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398888} {:answer/id #uuid "c04d34f3-85b9-5e8c-b8f6-dc61a84a62ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889} :answer-field/id #uuid "69629315-77cc-510c-b6be-75d926e5d966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2058ea27-d95b-5f37-8b22-5c724bfe6f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49468" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}s=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=s+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2/5}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(s+1)}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[{(s+1)}^{2/5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s+1)+0 \\\\  & ={(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "5851d6e2-d46e-5f7e-8a20-cfb7107da393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(e,\\sqrt{2})$ and  $\\left(\\frac{1}{e},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399046} {:answer/id #uuid "3d50a6a9-757b-54ed-a3ea-ef9ca589d5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and  $\\left(\\frac{1}{e},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399047} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3fd924-6b61-5f7a-b8ee-202868ddd4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{e},\\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399049} {:answer/id #uuid "57f28662-75ae-5e7c-a727-6cc70f2a946d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399338} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc1b791-33fe-5cbe-8db2-7cec317b3415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399339} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a22d-6748-5635-9bff-c054c9d5412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399340} {:answer/id #uuid "a32a5598-4875-50b4-a207-219749ce447a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399341} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea83b4-6e8e-5133-9645-9dd52053a38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399338} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdb2771-62f0-54fa-9623-4972845d19a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} {:answer/id #uuid "29258b68-aef8-521d-bcd0-4ced0913a87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470643} :answer-field/id #uuid "84edb204-5bb6-5b15-bce3-ab37bb9167a4" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471827} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "671dc28a-4f0a-5152-a704-c6f85c3291ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470645} {:answer/id #uuid "86897aab-f9f4-534b-a305-6d708d258191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470646} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7980c7-726d-5b91-94fd-76106f224eca" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a0d9e7-b5e7-588d-816d-4ab61e18e992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} {:answer/id #uuid "e352800d-a7f4-56b9-b333-aa4cd4f14a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470647} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1f5d5e-e005-55bf-8ec5-9de9792267ea" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79e7ca1d-1ed8-5888-8523-0ec8ed90db1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101736" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}\\right]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{1}{x}\\right]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1e039d-033d-5fb2-afe2-058a41532346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399386} {:answer/id #uuid "b5518e7e-ad19-5fdf-b048-f6f07aa85005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399387} {:answer/id #uuid "a777899f-4fb2-599e-8bac-fd1080f27455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} {:answer/id #uuid "730ca820-682a-52b2-87ed-4d379934e772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399389} {:answer/id #uuid "89a3a902-db89-527e-ac98-aebc7e8b5c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399388} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aed52a-be07-5913-ae72-4601a0fc9425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c3c2591-89b8-5e94-a7b0-e02f5b424693" :question/math-academy-id "q-103125" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(1+\\frac{2}{n}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},\n$$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b34439c-2388-5711-9ac8-63f5bf3cf549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(-7,0)$, radius: $49$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399414} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb48f02-d998-5b04-842c-b869161eadb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399415} {:answer/id #uuid "317341c2-5258-5001-88cf-9b017288ada7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399416} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bcc4ce-6ce1-5ebf-8946-9df961f73342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399417} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed03bf8-4e63-5262-8883-5b8d87a82e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(7,0)$, radius: $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399415} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c479e6f-23bb-5fd5-ab28-ff2cfcd0fb64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f6a0f67-fd62-5333-8099-37761d6fc395" :question/math-academy-id "q-103892" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+7x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+7x & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+7x)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\left({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right)+{y}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {\\left(x-\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$ and its radius is $\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb48f02-d998-5b04-842c-b869161eadb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399415} {:answer/id #uuid "317341c2-5258-5001-88cf-9b017288ada7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399416} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bcc4ce-6ce1-5ebf-8946-9df961f73342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $\\left(\\frac{7}{2},0\\right)$, radius: $\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15350554-e886-5e11-8207-77bbb3e0bec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.93\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399421} {:answer/id #uuid "fb654c8f-375d-5fa0-b333-4fd2c3d1805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.72\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399422} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6c956a-c96c-5420-a899-8859c8a907f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17.35\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399423} {:answer/id #uuid "3a202375-39cd-502a-81cb-6cca990bb170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.06\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399424} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bc53a0-6be4-5825-a047-05acf7782f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16.08\\,{m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9367409-a53f-579f-8cdb-a83c5faaa2a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1af9295c-5eff-510e-ba44-71a6e28cc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104195" :question/problem "A gardener wants to fence a rectangular flowerbed with $15$ meters of fencing. To two decimal places, what is the largest area that he can make with the fencing?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](images/be/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $15.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x\\left(\\frac{15}{2}-x\\right) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} {:answer/id #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399471} {:answer/id #uuid "6299c235-6bfa-5841-89fe-0705634d245b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399472} {:answer/id #uuid "2d302c69-b32c-57f8-ae7a-4cd8d92c0343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399473} {:answer/id #uuid "d62b096f-ff9c-5138-8170-e9ee40a546da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} :answer-field/id #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105281" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y &=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=\\det ({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right),$ we get\n\n$$\nH\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\det \\left({f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\right)=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)>0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)={h}_{11}=1>0.\n$$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} {:answer/id #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399471} {:answer/id #uuid "6299c235-6bfa-5841-89fe-0705634d245b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399472} {:answer/id #uuid "2d302c69-b32c-57f8-ae7a-4cd8d92c0343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399473} {:answer/id #uuid "d62b096f-ff9c-5138-8170-e9ee40a546da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63ab9ff-56cc-569e-a2eb-37c7b01b50d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399519} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb712d9-a5c5-5d8c-831a-ab2ad2eb58be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399520} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e7b9b8-1b51-5d9a-a752-82b801722063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399521} {:answer/id #uuid "39a8fbcf-5380-5a95-af67-4ee1a6d74327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399522} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd1cb9-8166-5d71-80af-8f3412ded7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399521} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e2b45d-5b9d-5091-9941-06bd82a064b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a0cb1f4-667e-57bd-8d19-2b32f34f6a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106585" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,0)-f(-1,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,1,0⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,0,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x+1 & =y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t-1\\,\\,y=t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t-1\\,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-1)+3(1-t)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-1)[2+3(t-1)] & =0 \\\\ (t-1)(3t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}-1=-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =1-1=0, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(0,1,0).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ab9ff-56cc-569e-a2eb-37c7b01b50d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399519} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb712d9-a5c5-5d8c-831a-ab2ad2eb58be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399520} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e7b9b8-1b51-5d9a-a752-82b801722063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399521} {:answer/id #uuid "39a8fbcf-5380-5a95-af67-4ee1a6d74327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399522} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd1cb9-8166-5d71-80af-8f3412ded7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6773b545-4c85-529f-8c68-3b883278b649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399659} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a96fe-942a-5e12-a66c-9e48bde3cb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} {:answer/id #uuid "fe66776a-5049-554f-b295-b90565404145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399661} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a7e53-ea54-5b25-9e84-5594638cadcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399662} {:answer/id #uuid "f849933c-eec0-5349-a930-557222d316e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} :answer-field/id #uuid "782ea541-65bb-5fe7-a7fe-a730b04302d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-109158" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z\\right) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$\n\nUse intervals with $x\\ne0$ and $C-x^3\\ne0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside the requested nonzero family."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b361b7f6-2975-5ed2-b3d6-cb981bcbc5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399762} {:answer/id #uuid "af436aca-c25b-50d1-ae25-749ecc05a446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4e5843-97cc-5c3b-b643-9e3130891ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399764} {:answer/id #uuid "04e15661-c522-5a93-a20e-dafb0bf87524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f1bfdb-7442-5063-835b-afe41546d21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eaa71aa3-e92b-5db0-96a6-7043b42bf47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112524" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x\\right) & =5 \\\\ 4\\left[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\right] & =5 \\\\ 4\\left[{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\right] & =5 \\\\ 4\\left[{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}\\right] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1f564d-015d-531a-84ba-220e72e5068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399783} {:answer/id #uuid "5cefa02b-c23b-5120-bf50-f5e584021c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399784} {:answer/id #uuid "64a8fd7a-b8ce-5f62-8477-ec3c16462673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399785} {:answer/id #uuid "2a57eba1-1adb-5f47-ab81-1ede087ed451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399786} {:answer/id #uuid "f73c86ea-5390-5440-876a-85ab356720c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399784} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0eba56c-3f43-5bc0-93ac-9b6c1e733e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6076d1df-0a51-5be4-8eb1-e4f9aaadd8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113176" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{6}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fa6bba-8366-50e3-bd6e-0487665ca2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399887} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba675a-9578-5d7e-86a6-668cee3df4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399888} {:answer/id #uuid "73d0aee8-773e-58fb-89a9-36098d20a922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399889} {:answer/id #uuid "d14d98e0-0f4f-5aa7-b716-c80926e97a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399890} {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a898ac5-9ac8-57ea-9a3a-e0a38e6b53ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a02e3599-0d24-591c-985f-c45e9f652ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115364" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3af3101fd7a103e8382755177ca1ebab9bc1cdf48aebd34af8ac7cc92991387c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1,4)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =\\left(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{6}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =(0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,3)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-0)}^{2}+{(2-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ac38215-9ec1-50f8-98ae-b02676f36ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470871} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd08602-c5a7-560d-a008-5af100e5acba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470872} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d53f74c-1edb-51e7-ab51-fa1d6a863e5b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471941} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0e2e90-5a0a-58ac-9406-4d7f380b59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470873} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0f0c5a-d11a-51d3-8073-4921d3e192bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470873} :answer-field/id #uuid "219c5856-1a99-5805-aa50-fa095a30b94c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19abd4cb-028f-5ec6-94bc-46e0531c7ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470875} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b3596f-4368-58cb-8a86-aa3574d44b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470875} :answer-field/id #uuid "07fa8bfa-32a8-579c-9f2b-d7e2020c6166" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "53ab2db1-0308-5084-926c-c3a100e39237" :question/math-academy-id "q-116038" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7000c9b2360173996e462883bdf9f89af674b9f099f25a866cd95bd286d9150.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/5f/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1\\right)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "774b6c52-6cd9-5494-9467-b267eeb3bf87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470877} {:answer/id #uuid "5f569ea2-a19d-574e-9fe7-3263cc5af081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470877} :answer-field/id #uuid "311a3fb5-e8a3-5661-879f-c32af7cf458f" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471944} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2717f896-3702-58fa-889c-ea1fa87b2751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470879} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cdc76a-3a05-5052-9840-7b3cfee62e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470880} :answer-field/id #uuid "5686f6ae-1d6e-5794-8379-cf0ad204f8d5" :answer-field/key "$(0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf51da3f-70bc-5207-8278-33335abafcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470881} {:answer/id #uuid "8210f463-a1a5-55dc-a7fe-8e20a153bfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470882} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0a676d-7f3c-5b04-a67d-49264c55020c" :answer-field/key "$(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b17707f8-d0a8-5f70-818e-f01d27903349" :question/math-academy-id "q-117010" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce3be13-af83-5bec-8118-2fb518752ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470883} {:answer/id #uuid "cc416fff-cf6e-5f61-8b8e-9967282b9712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470883} :answer-field/id #uuid "8246c5d0-b779-5ceb-8f21-287740210369" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e7bd50-f070-5b44-9a1f-b882aedac30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470885} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfbcb5e-fa2a-54e7-b7a9-11cfd12d361a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470886} :answer-field/id #uuid "0299bc50-a63b-52f8-96e3-e49ffd1a7ed8" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82de9881-8f89-53ce-a945-2c61011e3b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470887} {:answer/id #uuid "591a36aa-b4d8-59ec-9386-57e04fee0daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470887} :answer-field/id #uuid "820732f5-1133-5666-bdce-965a40cfec96" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d72740e-6072-5046-bfdb-c5667ec877d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117012" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "738c53f5-f5c8-5fab-bbb8-24d62bfe2a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399949} {:answer/id #uuid "32676595-fa5b-5541-bdb9-a9971fe141be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(3,\\frac{7}{3}\\right)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399950} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d5a1de-738c-52d2-83a4-ee63afd539ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399951} {:answer/id #uuid "d94ef636-d502-57eb-a2ff-477e7a72cf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399952} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba0a14c-37f3-5fb2-874e-33cf3105a4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(3,\\frac{7}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399952} :answer-field/id #uuid "709ede91-db66-5be7-a01a-f474f7dacadb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f9516424-4bd5-5885-82ea-4588d28a4cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117247" :question/problem "![](images/51/5180dfe04f31f43fdcdfa8bd720951df0c6fb0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "738c53f5-f5c8-5fab-bbb8-24d62bfe2a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399949} {:answer/id #uuid "32676595-fa5b-5541-bdb9-a9971fe141be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(3,\\frac{7}{3}\\right)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399950} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d5a1de-738c-52d2-83a4-ee63afd539ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399951} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba0a14c-37f3-5fb2-874e-33cf3105a4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(3,\\frac{7}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc1fd11a-5a3a-5cae-bc26-e3f11bed8250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470895} {:answer/id #uuid "38544f56-a752-565f-b142-efc6dd34eff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470896} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4704532-a99c-50a6-bc34-d80150890f92" :answer-field/key "$(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af54704-1eb3-54d7-b7f8-119da98bd623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470897} {:answer/id #uuid "5a18a8d2-8505-50d1-afc4-88dd6cf30f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470898} :answer-field/id #uuid "da6b40a6-e64e-5d5f-b1fa-1bb9cc1269a3" :answer-field/key "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b7aeec-9d82-5096-b408-2c21e8eebfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470899} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04d5c1-e106-513e-a70b-70e993921a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470899} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef80172d-e2dd-5d28-ad7c-7f046bab6e53" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d42d29a1-7ec6-555e-8ef7-3216eb563375" :question/math-academy-id "q-117369" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1712079f-0724-55c4-bcf9-4a641811e473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399977} {:answer/id #uuid "964eca01-2ae2-551e-a658-79835ac5c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399978} {:answer/id #uuid "784750f3-6750-5c52-9edd-ecd848bdea7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399979} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cac279-8da9-592d-88fb-73f8fe0b7a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399980} {:answer/id #uuid "83bfd300-7ebf-55e8-b278-1ff27d7a64f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399980} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47ad7cb-b24b-5fa1-931d-75679414940e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61ffdc01-8414-55ad-b1b4-42608bbe9957" :question/math-academy-id "q-117737" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/40/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b6602d-f037-52b0-8c48-d0885dedafc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x+9{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399991} {:answer/id #uuid "30b063ed-08ab-5694-9771-9427e9a07c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x-2{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} {:answer/id #uuid "95477359-7c43-51b4-a1ff-890600150acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x+{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399993} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2d9c2f-bf87-54d1-a5c5-0103894f75e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x-18{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399994} {:answer/id #uuid "3694a7a7-cd54-5712-bdcb-421abc079139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x-3{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f36605a-43ff-547f-a708-ab078c73b400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e422fe9-fc97-5df0-b6a5-ce1fd6f9f5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-119037" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)-6{e}^{-3x}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+6{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+6\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+6\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\right) \\\\  & =x-2{e}^{-3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb841386-1180-59be-8b8f-ef4a3bdf4bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400060} {:answer/id #uuid "e0471f0f-25c1-5626-b98b-3b2fe326dc7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400061} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed50d71-47ef-504d-b078-392fbb23bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(4t+1)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400062} {:answer/id #uuid "303677bb-cada-51a9-b7bf-74bbee38a830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{2/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400063} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddf2ba5-5cf7-5f47-befa-2cacf9a12596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400061} :answer-field/id #uuid "56af02a6-0883-5dcb-9c23-0114fa511fa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "528f1204-b150-5ae3-b3f2-1df94a35f90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122351" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ee/ee516744d915553bf962b498af522cf0006d42c5d0e0e2a003e6f385dd069e4a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8\\sqrt{0+1}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\sqrt[4]{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/4} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{3{C}_{1}}{4}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/4}=3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{4/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{4/3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57570228-8466-52cb-a11f-f04e18706442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470931} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8642b5-04f8-5c37-a14b-aed7ee7a7659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffa6fff-c4b5-5b71-b3d9-ad640d9a3224" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d09e71-31ad-54bf-8cea-1a8f785b490a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470933} {:answer/id #uuid "022b26c2-7d25-5b06-a964-476aa350c72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470934} :answer-field/id #uuid "528c0afa-a7a6-540b-be79-d029b67039c3" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6fcdbe-5b61-5426-9e91-ddd477fc741b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470935} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe5aeb4-873c-584e-ac06-a0748a68d7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e500961e-1c74-5a51-95f4-6ce13652944a" :answer-field/key "${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471973} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b39e29b9-45de-54bf-802c-fc56e0e1af24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668999} {:answer/id #uuid "bf807b7c-1463-58c8-8f77-2609ba389767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668999} :answer-field/id #uuid "84637aa4-ab55-514b-b9ce-bcab32cb8c23" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4187c6ec-17d9-5b83-9a20-6aff01cc94de" :question/math-academy-id "q-123315" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\left(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$”,  the limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$” diverges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484017d2-f86d-53d7-ba7b-17877ef8618f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400102} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d356bc-0293-5d2b-8823-146a5682e27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400103} {:answer/id #uuid "32df7dc7-96c6-574d-a78c-a84f8f49fddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400104} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbf65d1-e958-54ce-8140-5842c29862df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400105} {:answer/id #uuid "f8888596-d7ab-5567-adcf-d438bdbdd0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cccdfc-23c3-5334-80a8-20fa60433aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d56bb6b-dd41-54d2-8847-130e4a93bbfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123331" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{({n}^{3}+1)({n}^{3}-1)}{n(2-n)}$ for $n>2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{({n}^{3}+1)({n}^{3}-1)}{n(2-n)}=\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{6}-1}{2n-{n}^{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{6}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left({n}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{n}-1\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence diverges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b515481-27aa-50cd-a614-2b3b3c8e9916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400235} {:answer/id #uuid "70cb914f-6e67-52a8-8099-833f6e52d19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400236} {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} {:answer/id #uuid "a615fbac-40a1-5a96-905b-a1b1e6c1fe27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400238} {:answer/id #uuid "92822cb5-bcbd-5e16-981e-b28bb3550efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1c6021-571a-58eb-ae1a-ae2e4f0fcf7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e567d72e-c85f-58b7-9ff5-c8e3c41c5382" :question/math-academy-id "q-126774" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{3x}{{1}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x{n}^{3/2}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =3|-x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{3},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.   So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}{\\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right)}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\left(1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\right)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.   So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $\\left[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right].$"} {:answer/id #uuid "246bb1dc-7057-5890-bb32-5ae992573b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400237} {:answer/id #uuid "92822cb5-bcbd-5e16-981e-b28bb3550efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606b9a5e-55ed-5e60-92f5-b47ee5f67ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400242} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dd58b2-db2a-5f8e-8dd4-53e78bdbc1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400243} {:answer/id #uuid "857fe3f7-9574-5a04-bb5d-26945b5b3ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400244} {:answer/id #uuid "d79b2f0b-232c-51be-8226-2f1f3340e84c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400245} {:answer/id #uuid "34207664-1cc3-511a-9275-7f215d28db11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400242} :answer-field/id #uuid "15cc7274-0fbf-5ba4-9530-45869135aca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b063a87b-7453-5eed-82c3-d4c7e3cbe51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126838" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a35b6d-2cae-582e-8493-f87cca09de5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400249} {:answer/id #uuid "89946061-3179-5a62-92a4-c8e5a2b9e7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400250} {:answer/id #uuid "4d287fc9-fb22-5b2f-ba9a-16affbe14ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400251} {:answer/id #uuid "09e9c576-9966-5982-b9d6-7c94b62a04c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400252} {:answer/id #uuid "c517880b-005f-5788-8339-983446cb7ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400251} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d09d6d-fdd3-564f-8577-1f47d907f393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1276b48a-33df-5447-9033-1c8e5ee23a8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126841" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813ea027-299b-510a-86a9-2e76ce0edeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈0.739" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400270} {:answer/id #uuid "81e7917c-2e81-580f-949d-985ad24febfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈0.735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400271} {:answer/id #uuid "aee0f806-2311-54bd-9a9b-bb6e04c3b17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈0.744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400272} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4972b2-7545-5695-be8e-1e853fe55f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈0.746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400273} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2db0c1-d929-521e-913f-2d2d27ccf71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)≈0.732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4855ece8-350c-52ea-af76-5afe755310a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) & ≈1+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\right)+2{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5c6186e-dc96-51d6-82cf-5c90683fa84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439503} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd21dd5-0725-5fc9-a948-255a7375e687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae52bae-eb88-5ee0-a6c0-ddf96c9c5413" :answer-field/key "$(-3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f138de-76d3-51c6-b70f-37f4ae000dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} {:answer/id #uuid "abd8a918-fae9-5155-b1e4-9387ec218bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea60b4f-c7d5-5ba9-b54b-a47a64f292ec" :answer-field/key "$(-3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707f2204-105d-5f98-a7c5-02aaedc7a057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} {:answer/id #uuid "d8586660-fd7e-5eda-9284-01ae5a7a40b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} :answer-field/id #uuid "192ed7e0-867a-5042-9244-595a5145fa06" :answer-field/key "$(3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7553f1-861f-51c0-8547-71189b4ae228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} {:answer/id #uuid "3f812865-94f7-5778-aa60-73b89c449256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd2c2d4-80d6-5681-8a2f-d724cd733e7a" :answer-field/key "$(3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a633d445-62c8-5864-b7bf-d694cd1cd56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128519" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1\\right)i-\\left(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1\\right)\\mathbf{i}-\\left(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}\\right)\\mathbf{j}=0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 &=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56c5895f-0312-5a1b-aa81-74432f1a677a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472634} {:answer/id #uuid "d57966ac-2f7c-5bc4-a8c5-65630a1d5758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472634} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be720c7-5295-5e04-92ab-bc14f518e6df" :answer-field/key "${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3182dc74-62d1-5167-9fb3-faf55e9da825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472636} {:answer/id #uuid "778f926c-1c30-52dc-9b5b-093672a84639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472637} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef1686d5-f2b2-56da-9484-96521f179d9c" :answer-field/key "${D}_{2}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8593b2a9-e446-54b2-b2ef-e7e11d7e793c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472638} {:answer/id #uuid "fefe0407-b32a-5fa0-a3d1-5fb9b7b3e4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472639} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4cd4326-93b9-5e74-b562-0ccf10e9684b" :answer-field/key "${D}_{3}(y)={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a8a1c12-7f84-5c3b-83f4-8db057221abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130256" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is **not** linear. To see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2}) & =a\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right]+b\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right] \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a\\left[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right]+b\\left[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a\\left[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right]+b\\left[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a45c661-0328-5b60-bf43-2805e6e2bdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472640} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9275d3-6d58-5c1e-8aed-a79464b0f60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472641} :answer-field/id #uuid "50304e5e-3802-559a-a25b-e235dace79f9" :answer-field/key "${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df01b709-9070-50ce-9ec7-eafc96b7fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472642} {:answer/id #uuid "ec36995e-5892-5d87-9fbe-0d9e8f9e9890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472643} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4c274f-4a5a-5381-ade3-6743e84b9acb" :answer-field/key "${D}_{2}(y)={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dde77a8-6b23-5244-bbc0-5609fee08c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472644} {:answer/id #uuid "c499ada1-6b3c-5d89-b482-ec6473f37078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472644} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae89a85-f0db-583e-90c1-cc35ca650aa2" :answer-field/key "${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+xy$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d8c3559-a0d3-57b0-8496-a5eb629e4fde" :question/math-academy-id "q-130261" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2ax{y}_{1}-2bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =a\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right]+b\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right)+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right)+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2}) & =a\\left[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d89fcce3-b832-5e4c-bf7c-02c1c17153f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400585} {:answer/id #uuid "fd55f488-68a4-5b1e-9bbb-f31e747d2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400586} {:answer/id #uuid "1facde6d-5525-541b-93d6-30090cb94ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400587} {:answer/id #uuid "fa042a55-7109-5f70-97f2-337042cc265e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400588} {:answer/id #uuid "be65614c-b0a6-53df-8fb4-0615b69a4d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400586} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b83c99-19f6-547f-98fa-adc91ffefc5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42598c74-a5de-588f-a397-c6d324d9491a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131273" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2c4fc2-a754-53f6-ab25-c364e85fb49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400655} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3b49b7-2177-58c7-9dfd-a4e06235e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400656} {:answer/id #uuid "046dbcd6-e3df-5486-9381-db7ac7f22442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400657} {:answer/id #uuid "946cdf15-ce31-5c87-88df-3ce8a6a5154d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400658} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebc0bb1-5876-5366-9608-8b5ebf54b913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400655} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e8d157-633b-58f4-9968-e3a163c79d0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e19d212-99bc-5ec6-ba7f-76b0f4f4e8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132204" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩,$ since the point $P(0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66512e2-661f-5bb9-a7ee-3f76eb96d1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400662} {:answer/id #uuid "a43f9535-88b8-5223-a7ce-2e008288589b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400663} {:answer/id #uuid "c73a8a16-83c6-597c-bfd3-b995e0a8d790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400664} {:answer/id #uuid "da64ed04-930d-5f69-8aa5-1534bad7fdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty \\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400665} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0f1ee6-4f9d-5784-96cb-23288a1cac60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400664} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d6e03b-d7a1-55cc-9b86-b95900f2d614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e994daf9-6dc5-55a7-aade-020d8278b5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132666" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6)<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential function is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x-6<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & <0 \\\\ 4x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $\\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "c73a8a16-83c6-597c-bfd3-b995e0a8d790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400664} {:answer/id #uuid "da64ed04-930d-5f69-8aa5-1534bad7fdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in \\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty \\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400677} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400678} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679} {:answer/id #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3\\%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}\\right) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}\\right) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400683} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfc67ac-b865-541b-abd3-49ba35181b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$3.73$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400684} {:answer/id #uuid "7922a355-3dcf-5ee9-a956-87ba8d17b65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$4.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400685} {:answer/id #uuid "be6f8eda-cb13-5113-8a9a-1637beaf9d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$4.41$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400686} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddb9a99-6c2a-5cfd-b39f-b1b603fa424c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\$5.38$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400683} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f6174e-7ddd-5db0-b8a0-9648c670a8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-132856" :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $\\$0.7.$ In addition, in $94\\%$ of cases the admission fee is between $\\$0.75\\mu $ and $\\$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P\\left(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}\\right) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}\\right) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c893f0-7ed9-50d0-9479-4bfb6c5c6906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401106} {:answer/id #uuid "5e681a2e-8cd8-5ca9-b5d3-8777f4728c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401107} {:answer/id #uuid "075c1360-2993-54ea-a5ab-60fe6486955e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401108} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e5ed6-4c6c-5020-bbc5-7c83a63fb116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401109} {:answer/id #uuid "d396b6f8-3098-5eff-a463-bdfe23d65067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401107} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c104c61-200a-5174-998d-683d5dd55c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15805" :question/problem "![](images/de/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4\\right)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac04977-1a37-5d3c-9110-290be918a633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401214} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7eb081-9f2c-5f53-a5b0-e0278d5a43e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401215} {:answer/id #uuid "8b485d3f-8ad7-53a4-b507-d079c7107b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401216} {:answer/id #uuid "0e733731-6884-58f7-9e73-fde236398200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401217} {:answer/id #uuid "a04db056-3485-5f6c-b6b6-d2d4f8e80aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401216} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e4fe92-85aa-505b-a8c5-4df7656265ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right\\rangle =⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }\\right\\rangle =⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4fc5fe-cb91-578b-8dbf-4a5ffd8c3fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401249} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9077fe-1593-50c2-8f89-3d6b0dcabdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401250} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8485ee-e4b0-5c7e-96b8-4bdcc10e86bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401251} {:answer/id #uuid "09583e22-9aba-582a-80e6-e8808ccbeebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401252} {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee6b27a-bcd5-52e8-9493-e839011ba707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "417f7e3b-659c-5f7d-913c-decf384148f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161457" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂x}-{\\color{blue}Q}\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffab214-a531-5f16-9548-4aba64a14e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$∂z$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401256} {:answer/id #uuid "27be7768-bf01-5a25-8f76-a7b3aa6827e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401257} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b36ca-8d50-5d13-b2d4-01dff2d1189e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401258} {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} {:answer/id #uuid "401f8a2b-7596-5052-8ec8-449369280a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585e533-2d5e-5fa5-b53e-85ce1c70e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2128236-dd4e-5cec-8d28-c07554df0f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161463" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂x}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab621f-8cde-5ff7-83da-d05fc13356ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401263} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1077bb-f87e-5cca-9aef-16e4fb33aef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401264} {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} {:answer/id #uuid "6170ad11-316c-53bb-b473-67e1f8f56f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401266} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc272-4ba4-53bf-8e7e-06633eea609a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d473e4-fb62-570e-a504-252ea46fe0d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "682d2ef7-9cff-5a52-921d-70cd49bbb080" :question/math-academy-id "q-161476" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xy}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xy}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z\\right\\rangle {|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\hspace{0.222em}xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{y-x}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{x-y}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xy}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x}\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e\\right)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdff4620-93ff-550e-afdc-c665cb0060db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401297} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa29027-2d4f-5367-917f-52c169af3941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401298} {:answer/id #uuid "9a681f25-a3d3-54aa-8220-4047401f3201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401299} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb3ebdb-8986-5120-bc0e-293da834f2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401300} {:answer/id #uuid "ec03a2a0-b115-5ed7-a6b0-20a8edc98e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401298} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b9fac4-36f4-5471-8875-46834f232265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0831acd-ddeb-5e04-811c-4d36ad3d1496" :question/math-academy-id "q-161827" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f7ed51c1273b73243beb574535686f4ce3498b28c84cbe76833a3096674792c.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d3/d338181a6631c513330ef2dd3899cfb9b01cb6f4c4ff7d9cd65038b381e7fec4.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,3]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi \\right]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df4d06f-b85b-5df3-8dc8-2bb8f495582f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401311} {:answer/id #uuid "1a198055-107c-5684-9d14-e7c623094522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401312} {:answer/id #uuid "4925a5be-07f7-506e-b9ae-faf7c7f0dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401313} {:answer/id #uuid "83f96e24-8d6b-5eba-89cf-5696e34a9895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401314} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2c4ff-8c1b-548e-992e-27aec7e25032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401314} :answer-field/id #uuid "531e02f0-754c-57e9-98aa-5097314bafb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f21c0998-8903-5ed2-945d-a8c8324dedb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161902" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,y,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ and the plane $z=y-x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=y-x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-y\\cdot 1+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower cone ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-0\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\cdot \\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+(-x)\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78fb291c-c31f-5ac0-9d77-7a2e07aadd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401345} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc16458-ce9f-50c1-8fbf-48870b72fe4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401346} {:answer/id #uuid "1153c6c7-bf78-52f2-9e1f-684f7eccb765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({\\sin }^{3}⁡t+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401347} {:answer/id #uuid "17520325-a116-5295-9221-ca1e38e5c07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401348} {:answer/id #uuid "0447c179-7aef-50a6-824a-496d140ba7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401345} :answer-field/id #uuid "b978e6f7-e64f-5b80-8951-81925452e0ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5ac123c-3663-55fc-a172-6e4b0415bb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162414" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{},\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 0-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 1|}{{((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}{{({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sqrt{{({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because cosine is positive for $t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$"} {:answer/id #uuid "bd89ecad-0fc0-5ba5-a620-05712d3a1b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401448} {:answer/id #uuid "d50858b8-1fd8-5038-a72e-57fc3037d5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401449} {:answer/id #uuid "99d03f44-97f1-5754-9f11-086ea13d97fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-1,0)$ and $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401451} {:answer/id #uuid "4408e820-21f8-5788-8399-5558cbe467ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$ and $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f3d642-de3f-54d5-aede-eba21e11be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401567} {:answer/id #uuid "e9df0568-122c-5b6d-9b84-d5caa3cabe43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401568} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401569} {:answer/id #uuid "20209ee4-eb61-55e6-aa7a-3caa61c198e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401570} {:answer/id #uuid "73f81d60-d0cf-5e03-8299-56a8163c5850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401569} :answer-field/id #uuid "160e9ede-f9e3-593e-8295-6afb981450c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $\\left(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right)$ and $\\left(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "e9df0568-122c-5b6d-9b84-d5caa3cabe43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401568} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401569} {:answer/id #uuid "20209ee4-eb61-55e6-aa7a-3caa61c198e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401570} {:answer/id #uuid "73f81d60-d0cf-5e03-8299-56a8163c5850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401574} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401575} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} {:answer/id #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401577} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $\\left(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right)$ and $\\left(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401575} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0beb1ecb-7649-51d5-8923-8085f1d146bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401629} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401630} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d87b1b-17a5-523c-a973-f5e0ad287cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401631} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4f4094-0cfd-5ae4-8308-88f0b83b2c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401632} {:answer/id #uuid "946c0adc-baba-5359-a975-69b7a89f4838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401630} :answer-field/id #uuid "5088efea-7f9e-57d5-8f9c-14c688a0e06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "104d7814-9b21-55a2-864d-c61ec56f5c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17392" :question/problem "Given that $y=2{x}^{e}-\\ln ⁡x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{e}-\\ln ⁡x) \\\\  & =2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\left(2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =2e-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d42c473-b845-56f6-91ed-2f7569cdadf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from   $(1,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401903} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd13488-86ae-53ef-94b1-8445c40feae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401904} {:answer/id #uuid "2eaf5793-614c-59c2-8266-69fcc04e0964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401905} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea9769-6304-556d-88e7-17fabaf6e8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401906} {:answer/id #uuid "132e7131-949c-5374-87b4-fafc2b9fee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,2)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401905} :answer-field/id #uuid "47132c57-ee0e-5ec3-b95e-67a15913786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bd99021-9f7b-5102-9e9a-342e79f39167" :question/math-academy-id "q-225228" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,y=(u+v-1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,(u+v-1)^{2}+1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{v-1}{0+1},\\,(0+v-1)^{2}+1\\right) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,(v-1)^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=(v-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to \\left(\\frac{1-1}{0+1},(0+1-1)^{2}+1\\right)=(0,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to \\left(\\frac{0-1}{0+1},(0+0-1)^{2}+1\\right)=(-1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$\n\n![](images/69/69a2a8ab136360c01d1a17a58dc2fc4864ae2089330a551df913b200d923f087.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56be28be-e705-54a4-a778-9f6ec8ce939a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402265} {:answer/id #uuid "6aed1c1d-e07b-56b5-8813-9bc524e3b177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402266} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f5bf53-f4d6-5d6a-980c-1b00b877e968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402267} {:answer/id #uuid "413d3322-907e-5aaf-a499-45064137e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402266} :answer-field/id #uuid "6520f82c-2711-5a08-aef8-d05fa583c5ad" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0840c9bd-29c8-51d1-97c9-4c4a62931e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402270} {:answer/id #uuid "6426dfc7-965f-5694-8849-76b5475c6032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402271} {:answer/id #uuid "af28147c-5f72-550f-b281-517b8bce6252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402272} {:answer/id #uuid "08df2de5-a148-5277-89dc-2f4b6e34694a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402273} :answer-field/id #uuid "27113094-5e40-5dac-9419-bf117be37919" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff1a95df-b525-52a3-a9f3-14f8763557db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402275} {:answer/id #uuid "0965069c-ec53-552f-94e8-f41e248346e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402276} {:answer/id #uuid "374b6057-dfd1-5f85-921b-54b469594c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402277} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17fad2-853c-5665-b126-6ced9d2c13cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402277} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a3e9d85-8afb-532c-bc98-02a869ca1ca9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08cfe6b5-a35c-56e3-a8b6-b208ff4d131b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402280} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b6d9b-8a8b-5425-9396-e4c78607c4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402281} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f34d3a-cfa0-5e02-8320-e125baa30770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402282} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ac352-d46a-51ed-a992-d68e07a53b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402283} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69873a9-e21f-55cc-ba65-c7ed6f009b57" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de25ec60-135e-5b0f-93ec-2a89a92381de" :question/math-academy-id "q-343341" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=5x$ defined on $[-5,5).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $10$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n5x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-5,5).$ \nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}5t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\left[-\\frac{5}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{5}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{250(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "6aed1c1d-e07b-56b5-8813-9bc524e3b177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402266} {:answer/id #uuid "413d3322-907e-5aaf-a499-45064137e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8731841-813d-5c15-9844-bc300aded01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402286} {:answer/id #uuid "7d76bc51-9148-5c68-8eea-0c61f6eca229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402287} {:answer/id #uuid "4b83a754-c4bd-58d7-824f-3b576edd0dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402288} {:answer/id #uuid "152ee5ab-ddc5-54e1-abb5-85e05ecbc24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402287} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc21dfac-bf45-5494-902e-e23a4ab50c43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71c7a223-28dd-5b98-95b8-adb22dcd23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402291} {:answer/id #uuid "6acee3a9-dc08-557c-942a-b0c19f30c1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402292} {:answer/id #uuid "d530070d-72ef-5bc5-ad66-9f775c9da2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402293} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c4db87-8656-51bc-8c6d-365da5edd0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402291} :answer-field/id #uuid "98447180-9883-5216-9b4d-2f8d5e46c29a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402290} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acb22964-9386-55f3-b4ba-97747bec61c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2de29-17f1-5bf9-b1b1-74047f493c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402297} {:answer/id #uuid "c2980206-7d39-5dc7-9dd7-4a889845d7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402298} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab80b53-c5e0-529a-8043-c52969416199" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35eec229-a765-5483-a12b-af96b02f02b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402300} {:answer/id #uuid "c73e9609-34d2-5881-aad7-336bd7907eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402300} :answer-field/id #uuid "2793680d-49bf-5c9e-8ee3-c5e0e9eb684c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e361d1a0-a782-5eed-a0dc-4cde0324cd70" :question/math-academy-id "q-343395" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|x|$ defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}(2n-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}x\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0\\right)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}x\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1-\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0\\right) \\\\ 0 & =1-\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1 & =-\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f6f980b-262a-50f0-ae3d-21301e83956a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402304} {:answer/id #uuid "c9091f78-a863-5b79-82af-d78f1b7a6e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402305} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2405a2-3755-5df5-98e5-3e6b5d35f747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402306} {:answer/id #uuid "81d46c72-93bb-5c34-b05d-8f8e24f569a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402307} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea007838-d48e-5282-9c26-7807b59be905" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc209ef-e0e4-5126-a4d3-28477eac6672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402309} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a6dfee-6b51-51e4-878f-80f01834b392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402310} {:answer/id #uuid "89861f72-3267-5a53-91e4-553173ff8b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402311} {:answer/id #uuid "37400167-26ee-5154-ac19-3de49ebc5692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402311} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2df7cda-b6f1-52c3-94de-e01cfc959b14" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaad6fdf-5307-581d-a51e-86eb719eaa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402314} {:answer/id #uuid "1129b323-9145-5bfc-8573-2c423509d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402314} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabd5536-e333-5913-8a91-953c55edab0a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f13b00-9c43-5ce3-9ea2-b89ef353d34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402317} {:answer/id #uuid "5794e73f-ea5d-524c-bb11-82c1cf90b93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402318} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9629e5-251b-531d-8097-ef9b42974051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402319} :answer-field/id #uuid "240e5c16-d6bd-5404-b856-e008054e31c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ea02f42-e1ec-509f-a6b0-19fd2f4064ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-343438" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}\\right)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}\\right)=\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi \\right)=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}\\right) \\\\ -1 & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59e944d4-7bfb-518e-9bee-2fdf150f8144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402322} {:answer/id #uuid "47de1570-9f8f-50d0-b18a-c75ae926d19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402323} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcea2a2-3096-50e6-a6b7-e9b80e1e8393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402324} {:answer/id #uuid "c33a2222-b813-50a8-a34a-21f9fa0adc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402325} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fb0232-8fde-57f4-8464-e80e09aee727" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936b6cfe-f0ac-576f-a3dc-b5ba23f06fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402327} {:answer/id #uuid "70