423207} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7c78ab-810e-5194-923c-bb29e87bc1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423226} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a2e1c1-74f1-595f-8870-09a1c08cfd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423233} {:answer/id #uuid "e582708c-30ab-51b7-8fac-ed5cfb399655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423237} {:answer/id #uuid "e671311a-a6c6-5590-aa9c-c58e95336c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423238} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0d9dcc-3414-5481-a1ea-2423a23ed503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423207} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5dac63d-6b9e-5dfb-b42e-18dabc9a7e34" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d180264d-6aaf-5aca-a8a3-12aea15eb407" :question/math-academy-id "q-341785" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe constant coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)$ and $L=4,$ the constant coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant term of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186b12e8-0537-5623-ab6f-d5dfb2e0cdcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423196} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce23bf5e-8b29-5cf7-9b38-cc60d7fad1c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc8861f3-8c91-59c6-b70f-78e5364379e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-341817" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [0,\\pi ) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{4}.$\n\n${a}_{4}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi .$ Also, $f(x)=-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ and $f(x)=3$ for $x\\in [-\\pi ,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{-\\pi }^{0}-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08051b4d-e523-5380-b21a-12e9c0665d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423370} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce314fb-b190-50fc-b3d0-c8253bbb95a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423373} {:answer/id #uuid "1a03aa61-a6a4-59bd-9047-ec009e7bb552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423376} {:answer/id #uuid "a7023940-c9dc-5a8b-a379-2f0028725950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423400} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a70d9b-245d-5412-9927-59ddc777a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423424}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423376} :answer-field/id #uuid "919ef9fd-60f6-5c91-983a-1dea7afd83ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0aa0eff9-8245-5a13-9648-df63821931fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161216" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d333ab14f8a42bb1d40894421afc76ff60acbb6c4cb858ddee5f6e8a39be2d15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/51/5193451b39b03b11cf9780fd7039360ba8fb5de5876a3593f7a5dadde2e7be5b.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-1}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-0}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-8\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b26cc63-426c-571e-9b86-42c72dd116be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423381} {:answer/id #uuid "b585aa7c-e074-5288-bfb9-ef96a42c4a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423401} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ddf932-21f8-5717-80af-819313ab65be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423406} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eb1d49-0894-5e35-8da8-22eace89ec4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423407} {:answer/id #uuid "cda7553e-81c6-5b0b-b7ee-b9649cfb537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423401} :answer-field/id #uuid "bebafe40-b7bb-506e-8e35-eb97971e464b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "348cfda2-2f1c-590f-874e-27ed4639164d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161218" :question/problem "![](images/07/0785d19e62e5b317bbaee924fa8b6b303c2bbd10ab338357e6a88846b1d5b1d1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fd/fdaad0039f4b7fea5c6e301f4cc3b89687f5f4081e3bd87f8ce31daacda2744b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-2xy\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}9-6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[9y-3{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-9[y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6+(-18) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454b87df-9358-5e83-88c0-07680f41c488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423382} {:answer/id #uuid "4f178a77-2414-5214-8bd5-bd39a6eac81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e(1-3\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423385} {:answer/id #uuid "5551c287-c78f-519c-b510-9ad385ed9a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-2\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423388} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7f17a1-53a4-5fed-b5c7-d9ac1b2ba8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi (1-{e}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423389} {:answer/id #uuid "cc20ddd6-e49d-5338-a922-ad93aa42f23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423389} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e0a989-8025-59cd-b463-0e58f6dbbcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "541f1701-1958-5d60-973d-d311e60b511f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162541" :question/problem "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/00/0072a53c42916c095c625cebd0a0564b92dcac5469f9d7452e9c75ef7e7d7158.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨(3-3){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3-0){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022cc252-1b2d-53c8-b8f4-7e0fa0c560d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423367} {:answer/id #uuid "8e594166-996a-561a-a110-dc2f02ae4841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423397} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7f4c11-cb48-56a3-a231-fbfe74c38e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423399} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1cead4-2415-5cbb-975a-9e945db0ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423403} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6f293e-ee33-59bc-ae0a-421d6d6069d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423399} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c5df89-0a4d-58f0-9562-de84c7296eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5dff93a1-4655-5852-a4ef-0e50149062ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-161217" :question/problem "![](images/83/838fe13d4ad778c391ff628b508d17ed450b56e800180c946038be1ccfc5655e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/d4/d47102fe8d01acf93fe0e6ef9017e06412e843ea64584b9f4e7151214aeb9197.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x-y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy+6x\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{13}{2}-y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{13}{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}y-x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y-x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[xy-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =11+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e66adf-5d3d-50f8-bd05-476213ea7079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423379} {:answer/id #uuid "684630f4-73ec-5717-89dc-735d07771dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423392} {:answer/id #uuid "dd410492-acca-5af4-b697-930048beeb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423416} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2f4d8-a73a-5078-92cb-e52bc18e91d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423420} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5fd008-10bd-5ee2-bb11-b1004b46b242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423392} :answer-field/id #uuid "f747b354-6e66-5b1a-8d1f-bb7fe1e6ed70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74852653-8123-54e4-8cee-cb7081e03f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162542" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4af0d5c96e08d87ea884ec1f0d5c87e0ca220c54c2783fb299c100cad6582088.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/e4/e4488f9fbf8c16883ac2470544d5fadcb63b2bff362b25a41a8a70bfe48232a2.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8({1}^{2})({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =8{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =8{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =64\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨8({0}^{2})({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =64\\pi +0 \\\\  & =64\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184fb30f-a682-5501-8386-eb41dfbe9220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423374} {:answer/id #uuid "6747bbec-720b-52e6-99cc-9d109af4dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423391} {:answer/id #uuid "c158a00f-6972-5251-9b09-b9f5fb43ebc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423408} {:answer/id #uuid "fed859a1-00f2-56ee-a80c-c935c99c8ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423428} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5a674-7ecb-5030-bc4c-bee02665aeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423391} :answer-field/id #uuid "916a132b-ef75-5ddf-ad1d-ae5149411e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "886facf5-e284-5b49-82f5-9671e671cb20" :question/math-academy-id "q-162561" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/e2/e2d914cf61af684d1f2971ec680acc1a2a53a6a40c85e387728dfea8ebc94730.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x-2+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =x+2{z}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(2{z}^{2}-2)x\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{z}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}z\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(x+2{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x{z}^{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}z+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{3}{2}+18\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{39}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06134337-3698-5e99-b304-3c524f8d58ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423369} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b67ca6-6357-50a6-8510-6325cf38d5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423402} {:answer/id #uuid "d03aaa3c-1379-5383-ba5a-20ab61ae0bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423412} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfc6802-b302-519c-b456-4ec4b85afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423417} {:answer/id #uuid "e104162b-8025-5219-bed4-9b1e8c92a728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423417} :answer-field/id #uuid "723cf9c2-2b81-5164-9613-762892b1d534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b265113a-f521-5f72-933b-1132d7a6f831" :question/math-academy-id "q-161220" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8708a16a0f6db347acf2c1a82a602d2fa8ce60d4bb8369f1414eca3465e4e71.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8e/8ed6ffdeab54bc6888d245ef4f1c050c31e010c67dfa93aea365fc6cb523e382.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy-4x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y-3\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-3y\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}2x+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\left[y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{15}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253e9383-49a8-53b0-8d24-6a8dbb0d3f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423380} {:answer/id #uuid "8086102a-b853-545f-8109-277022565d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423394} {:answer/id #uuid "84710785-2a7f-5147-a528-99d84da23127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423395} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8b366c-7b46-5852-bf2e-27c619fddf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423404} {:answer/id #uuid "f04bf4db-8996-5572-9ef9-fba37950252e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423380} :answer-field/id #uuid "83518110-ea5d-5b6c-8080-6f96a5271b8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec4a413a-1560-589f-a588-2fce65f680ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-162539" :question/problem "![](images/14/14b1144f52dcacc094901a159bf54c04f00daccc85df7b117b2163ed327dcd4f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/61/614037b52ca1f60f03420019891af1004227e2e0f3b4a89d71adbdc68dadf1dd.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=2$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨0\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/97/97ada65e0942c57cfaae530a099d183ade03377955766c21707192cf534c57a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423457} {:answer/id #uuid "28df0d1a-86ac-5691-b099-21f856eccfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f2/f22991a62043a8fbd1e27ea16a103a830fa5e3874517e9c84d6b11f9dac0702e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423459} {:answer/id #uuid "2d848126-3113-59a0-965e-db5f69875346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/62/62156bad25518bbc8e9c324a2906ba804e6dc38edd37a752bd3092f2313ebea0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423461} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff779d8-2902-5dbe-9b08-74d03fa4ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dc641778741dd5fbe43c8edad8a34f033e898eb279a7fa680b44f75aaebaabda.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423469} {:answer/id #uuid "94357291-cfe9-5d56-86c6-275da88ed2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/5663dfd0afc5b44954f73064b69aedcd33b983755fc7a425f4b963b3bad665eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423457} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160923" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f370177dc8119044b74fd4923c3d4318d5b9e1b4de89b63967284ee1cd7794da.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/03dae0fbacc0339613580414c2217652b9ff6303788c7ca265deb3b1f755f4cd.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423456} {:answer/id #uuid "332853ed-2acb-5257-876f-53cb0dc7f6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423462} {:answer/id #uuid "33fa1164-e443-5847-962d-f5038cdc0390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a52a5fba437ff08657af756644f5d4569d35bd7598b92721423103d8c308ca2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423463} {:answer/id #uuid "493f0a23-3da5-598b-a8a0-9acfa743f9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/74/74d029bf33b8c0e978006a0fb066843abf4c2dd0fc4c8ea7d125ae53b1626570.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423465} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba7decd-30d3-50fb-b574-0b6278fb2388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/34be2d9120d93014b0d176aca946ebaf310ccdd574285c487c26a8fe00778412.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423456} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" :question/math-academy-id "q-161024" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a44852c606e67842a58ad1a696253e6e211816f8243beec8b3eea97ef42055a2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\right)\\right),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9148e7a1-c898-5010-a8da-e14468900021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474442} {:answer/id #uuid "8c509f0c-7869-50fd-97c8-0c39ad974d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474442} :answer-field/id #uuid "80fd21d2-d0e2-56a6-83b8-c52c5aa81167" :answer-field/key "The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035981b4-173c-59ce-a4cc-935ef26ceb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474444} {:answer/id #uuid "e09384a8-158b-5092-8870-775a085bfe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474445} :answer-field/id #uuid "a427db06-c7e8-5666-90dd-909a4adf6847" :answer-field/key "The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a494b7-3cef-5f9f-b5df-0019758840e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474446} {:answer/id #uuid "d9805b37-2717-5444-9336-eeda03de0a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474447} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f8696b-5d50-5511-a270-5cccd6dc0050" :answer-field/key "The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/math-academy-id "q-161029" :question/problem "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$” is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis”; “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7366f0-ea26-5a4d-ad99-5c479c1363cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c8/c8890d6bf89507aff0334f26d0b906f260b3d5e00db974cdef59faf17d5068b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423458} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0aeef6-20b2-5bbf-b82d-cf19738fc0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4cbda09d23f88ee9b7c87632180f10fbe0c678b3c8ac6b8b183bbc82fd43c774.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423472} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6dca9e-c430-5e66-b053-f24ceb1406ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/db/db98f6bc045f3f3a6bd7d22e061861b2d00182af6968b9562b59f22248d94f6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423473} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d258ef-d6de-5ab7-9099-98537e9ff4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/90/909d48b33e74f7bdc0bd18d639325a88f7313126cadee7f367183541897e231e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423479} {:answer/id #uuid "caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423484} :answer-field/id #uuid "a914c4df-a2e3-591c-b430-880a8ee5c996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-160924" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f21fa7abb989e8c2bf35552675f498969e30e1f42012fd615c00686d20cb01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png)"} {:answer/id #uuid "b1292071-77e9-579a-8b2f-0a1e4da37f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(r-\\left\\langle 0,0,\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle \\right)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107d2581-115e-5fcf-a7ba-4b8a4e067792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424021} {:answer/id #uuid "19b9c547-b3de-5a3b-af9a-1d4bd21add26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424023} {:answer/id #uuid "33ea49c6-ab8b-5f20-989d-636291cc3105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424029} {:answer/id #uuid "51df5392-9584-5939-887e-e5eb023f5973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424034} {:answer/id #uuid "534fce73-0f67-5e15-aa99-7cc2da173c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424023} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9e477c-a83d-55bd-a460-7bef9eab339b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0228a94-8a4a-532e-880c-3c74fb2a7fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96333" :question/problem "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ compute $P\\left(0\\le X\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\pi },\\, & -\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi , \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(0\\le X\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-0\\right)}{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c9587e-71e5-566c-a7c0-2ca7d8543d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424058} {:answer/id #uuid "44d46d21-8790-5b62-b822-b15cfffb758c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424060} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddc91ad-5eb5-580c-aec8-05f1a65ef396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424063} {:answer/id #uuid "77b11337-68cc-5792-80b0-2420a4a46582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424065} {:answer/id #uuid "f27e69cb-8e5b-5f27-b7bd-fd886daab64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424065} :answer-field/id #uuid "174c2ec8-e1f1-51fa-bb13-18381c6477ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3754a443-839d-5055-afd8-172b5271b8be" :question/math-academy-id "q-90386" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\le x\\le 3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}\\right)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26751660-b10c-52b5-b32b-8fb5a1bdd82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424057} {:answer/id #uuid "90ca78b7-c93c-5e6a-8cc5-7647d2cc2740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424068} {:answer/id #uuid "91379b67-0509-5582-a25b-36b84489e757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424069} {:answer/id #uuid "c385dfd9-ebdb-5db3-ac8a-7fa4bb79f925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424075} {:answer/id #uuid "cb89e563-276d-50d6-a32f-f52c04c6441b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424068} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb8932f-143b-578f-a0db-bb4cc2c246c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7821024d-c004-5d99-9790-91a65c2602fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130751" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=6x(1-x),\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 6x(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7a65b8-5911-5995-890e-26eb26de2d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{vw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424166} {:answer/id #uuid "cda476b6-e024-5c13-a813-a0217e4446ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424167} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf34300-0330-5f0e-9e12-24a74c4b83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424170} {:answer/id #uuid "fa885cf6-9f29-528c-8aeb-86e960fc0083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{w}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f602c505-c9b2-58a6-873a-3101dc02439a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bbf87425-cede-5e7e-870f-278bd2b11904" :question/math-academy-id "q-161808" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y},\\,v(x,y,z)=y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\left(0-\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{y} & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f33a63-c291-5841-be1b-4faff432cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424260} {:answer/id #uuid "438050ca-5554-5efb-b6ab-495501b5ed22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424262} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4c6e33-c46b-5d45-876e-4d7c5861b00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424270} {:answer/id #uuid "e7caf4ba-0364-56de-9d87-fa137b1cb670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424271} {:answer/id #uuid "fd40483e-6c18-50b5-b937-12944951fb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4522aec1-18fc-5a98-a19b-f2258fae7499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09ef39a3-aa47-5850-a2b1-1c368b15a626" :question/math-academy-id "q-2523" :question/problem "Consider the function $y={x}^{(2x+1)}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2x+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2x+1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\left(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{2x+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2x+1}\\left(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x+1}\\left(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}\\cdot x\\left(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}(2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ebb2cb7-894c-5ea1-8fe9-5d932e0eaec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424266} {:answer/id #uuid "bc944f96-6c8b-5c8c-9ec9-40c54f081a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424267} {:answer/id #uuid "c290b836-3e40-5e91-84ff-1cbd84d8ae28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424269} {:answer/id #uuid "e80dcef6-8cf3-58d4-8627-98be0f13b772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424272} {:answer/id #uuid "ead5149a-8ad0-50cd-aebf-6ea20a3a902d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424273} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da65985-bad5-5a30-9563-e72ed071e141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82f90d91-406e-5d3c-bce9-9c8235648ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138851" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(2x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\right) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090603b9-f435-5f7d-81b3-5fa7d70d5ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424261} {:answer/id #uuid "4902eb83-e8de-509d-9be7-218c3d06e15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424263} {:answer/id #uuid "57d95622-82b3-5393-b370-8d92f43e6f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424264} {:answer/id #uuid "60eefb25-daf9-5e08-b4ef-37256dd0bc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424265} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdefb81-a578-5bb1-b28c-f155bdc49d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424261} :answer-field/id #uuid "1814a4c4-4f4b-5074-b701-a59013925f9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc4aa46c-af83-51d5-a531-dab6715c6436" :question/math-academy-id "q-138807" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(3{x}^{2})^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3{x}^{2})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 6x\\right) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14619b02-c3bb-5be6-85af-30af1494c733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424298} {:answer/id #uuid "441c7356-d182-50d5-a1bd-8b54901c3f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424303} {:answer/id #uuid "7f18edf8-e5d2-5da6-9302-024ff289f750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424311} {:answer/id #uuid "91bb664e-dbb4-5152-b157-eaf96ebfee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{26\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424313} {:answer/id #uuid "f40e93e6-995f-501f-964e-89e8ceda688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321} :answer-field/id #uuid "64089c5c-af77-5ef9-b56b-0b6dff4fd9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45cc9fce-954c-547e-835b-7dec4ec6d636" :question/math-academy-id "q-161103" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\left[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\left(9-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a1b0ee-a4a2-5576-8c66-7aaec2ee0e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424424} {:answer/id #uuid "22b18ee1-002f-5c66-b260-413039c7fa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424426} {:answer/id #uuid "44ad6232-afa5-5eee-acd4-ac7923c6c4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424427} {:answer/id #uuid "54f9c040-8ec5-5672-a995-f18c78ec39cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424429} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d735a-e23a-5d22-b726-fc09edb321eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424426} :answer-field/id #uuid "f715ad10-8843-5e87-a878-4728db817271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "462a3ac8-3692-5123-988e-2869c674b5a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113098" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=-2{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=-2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=-2{b}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}\\right)\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-8{b}_{1}+6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-8\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}\\right)+6\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}\\right) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072b4d5b-a518-50b1-9fd5-372915316f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424444} {:answer/id #uuid "231a4e7e-143a-55ee-96ec-349dc902c4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424449} {:answer/id #uuid "a888cc31-d112-55bf-8f2c-cc635b42c57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424481} {:answer/id #uuid "e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424493} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef2656-ec3b-5442-90ba-703850ba3398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424493} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e0080e-3bc6-5b4f-ad81-a8b266b3aa68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f963f5f-3418-5f15-a044-1af0d45a6d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127305" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0bf7b88eadd199edc1997410b7ddbaa74b204e7d0f8afef7c9b17c28d8f8dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{x}^{2}-4yx]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({1}^{2}-4y(1))-((-1)^{2}-4y(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-8y\\,dy \\\\  & =-4{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-4(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8a5a99-44bd-5b11-83dd-6c16e9e4e40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424478} {:answer/id #uuid "a581e533-3d41-5f80-8a3e-7a4d97fd2b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424480} {:answer/id #uuid "be728807-7dfc-57ad-844a-74242eaae230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424486} {:answer/id #uuid "f1954043-6aaa-5ee4-807d-74a521f1268a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424499} {:answer/id #uuid "f65df826-8fea-532c-b10f-fd1044e7f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424486} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5abcf2-857d-5997-8843-79c2143f0e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "380026fe-2d4e-5527-8902-7fe90302976f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127302" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨14xy,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=14xy$ and $Q(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{y}^{2}+6{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{x}^{6}+6{x}^{5})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{7}+{x}^{6})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3452acaf-89b6-53ae-861d-c8309d517e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424451} {:answer/id #uuid "38444180-4e68-5f1d-94e7-63374144fcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424452} {:answer/id #uuid "395d167e-4282-5829-b24d-6655ea33a853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424453} {:answer/id #uuid "4b779ef3-0c88-57d7-943a-271c2a83d88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424456} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424491} :answer-field/id #uuid "19474be3-1fd4-58b1-9c1c-f483db0b1748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "44ac1bd4-9843-582f-80a5-1b691b1d5da4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127328" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\hspace{0.222em}xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424446} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424460} {:answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424476} {:answer/id #uuid "db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-127337" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\left[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y\\right]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1)\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036be383-69ad-5c54-97f4-245cc64ebdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424443} {:answer/id #uuid "84832c07-1cf7-5890-95f5-93379bbcf9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424470} {:answer/id #uuid "a513c7fb-0fe0-56ce-955a-00f68e2e1226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424479} {:answer/id #uuid "ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424482} {:answer/id #uuid "eb98dc31-4f54-5896-a0d6-e621b6d1e1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424482} :answer-field/id #uuid "501779b1-729b-5f0b-8f00-ddfd19deba04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "621adbef-22f1-5cf5-b77b-1bf6fb7f602e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127326" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7400787e09600d370fa5c554e616fad6ba3a126a09725cfb236502f81a7e70e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4xy-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(-4(1)^{2}y-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4y-6{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[-2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3})-(-2(-1)^{2}-2(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2153ce06-d6d5-5127-8a6c-97309bd972d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424448} {:answer/id #uuid "29c7cf32-a75b-5b72-bb10-1df951e308a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424450} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe0b5de-0918-5eb2-8f3c-3bb3e4a8b13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424465} {:answer/id #uuid "85eceb92-6af4-585c-adf1-d11f60bbf317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424472} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de078e-efd8-5984-b3ca-2569a1db93a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2x(2+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424448} :answer-field/id #uuid "17417f10-d5b1-59a3-bbd0-62b09d7aaa49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8639ec93-c7f5-5ea1-8df7-3159f8c54b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127232" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,-1),(1,-1),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(6xy+y{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/86/861b873005c03ea6c25255f2febc975ff153d939780ec46e4f4503cbb50640dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6xy+y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6y+2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6y+2xy)+(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091bc970-fa77-5e71-85b9-f1ab8c6a0482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424445} {:answer/id #uuid "491c7d85-23b8-5360-a8a5-150039e21ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424455} {:answer/id #uuid "55384a96-bb48-572a-9f44-39ec987e63ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424458} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d61b2f-57fc-5cda-b0b0-9b59235e0fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424488} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d825d3-bc21-53d9-9b80-37d93f21f53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424495} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52f83e4-8134-5921-a35f-e8152b063a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d4f7d2e-5a54-5483-93ff-febe106e9483" :question/math-academy-id "q-127370" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/72/728036ffc90e2476017b3102b6279f9c5900f0e7fc58536e76c9aa038eb04f02.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y+1\\le x\\le {e}^{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}+y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\left[{e}^{y}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\left[\\left(e+\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right)-1\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c4895c8-33dd-51f2-b0a9-62d7e0bb7e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424457} {:answer/id #uuid "69428f30-71a1-5d98-bab5-a1556421d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424464} {:answer/id #uuid "7043ade4-6766-5180-8bd2-76e0d272838a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424466} {:answer/id #uuid "c1dcad37-cd08-51d7-ba24-ddf20a23ed67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424487} {:answer/id #uuid "e12e0d19-bdc2-57b6-9364-8156f4f5ea08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424464} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a624492-2c9c-5aeb-9ae8-4412b203d919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dccc7f7f-0cab-5758-b1da-5b22069d8a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-127203" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/0b/0b79306d0e78da924aa31b3bf1c73b7430ca322abc7f5a9b3107bb0a4d662182.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3-{x}^{2}y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5({x}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424463} {:answer/id #uuid "8581b009-276f-5642-a422-5b78da828fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424471} {:answer/id #uuid "89bd431e-528a-55dc-b640-1fbdebadacfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424473} {:answer/id #uuid "be2b7276-0419-561c-a09e-7c016dde655a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424485} {:answer/id #uuid "fad12fdf-85ee-5681-8fee-a7e21e160d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({x}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424463} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f093a1f-fd70-5b60-bb5c-a3a70624352c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec31d0f0-f633-5965-938b-954592f5e7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127226" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/63/636bfd79297e6fef4b0e56af9b0cb22f2e9a8061e833ea1dcf07e7958b870cdc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}+5)y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=5({x}^{3}+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424461} {:answer/id #uuid "799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424468} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424469} {:answer/id #uuid "974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424475} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-127307" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209d3320-50d9-50ef-9a25-a823b828f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424447} {:answer/id #uuid "3eed97bf-f19a-5ae8-9a9c-33659aaf6987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424454} {:answer/id #uuid "5c52dcea-70d7-560b-8792-445eff50c6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424459} {:answer/id #uuid "8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424474} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9c71a-e752-5728-b94c-b39be38cdb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424474} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f6f773-72e6-553b-a49d-36c27b57974f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fec27738-a1e3-5883-83b7-8672ecd1a476" :question/math-academy-id "q-127224" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9472021d3973167a7601785803837627476a4e8b1460e42f87e91ecb99d7e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016959b9-f05f-53b3-afd3-07e994e53882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424525} {:answer/id #uuid "17f972c0-e3f4-509a-b28c-755fd028d943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424529} {:answer/id #uuid "8012b58b-be32-5887-bb98-32352b39ab18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424546} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2d906f-f782-5277-a23b-1b4659529d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424555} {:answer/id #uuid "c54104af-76de-56d8-9541-8d02ef24fb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424555} :answer-field/id #uuid "13285fe0-63a3-5aa6-b3a7-7b2fe9fce9be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15d3a8f7-57e9-5955-b347-e7b15292f88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-253935" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(3),$ calculate $P\\left(X\\le \\frac{5}{6}\\,|\\,X>\\frac{2}{3}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a random variable $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} (\\lambda ),$ and two positive real numbers $s,t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3,$ $s=\\frac{2}{3},$ and $s+t=\\frac{5}{6}.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=(t+s)-s=\\frac{5}{6}-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(X\\le \\frac{5}{6}\\,|\\,X>\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(3)\\cdot (1/6)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc40c2d-1af0-5adc-a2bc-6557e8adc194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4683" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424533} {:answer/id #uuid "31011ded-b236-51be-8752-9a8a39f1aa2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4836" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424534} {:answer/id #uuid "3a08e9d4-be37-5bcd-b93a-f75705061f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4886" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424535} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6f8763-9d72-5754-bbed-ce927bc4b0f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424537} {:answer/id #uuid "47bc7314-1895-551e-8d2a-b165d99e961e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4386" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424540} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc8a138-558e-5ca1-bd83-4c4ce4806474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4b3ef3ac-d2db-5eb8-8c5f-a033b26be69b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132179" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{3}),$ calculate $P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.4386\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424603} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} {:answer/id #uuid "79d50006-6f06-56c3-bce2-b65f3e1eb21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424607} {:answer/id #uuid "d37fe04f-4a91-5f51-9524-f7477c2a7f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424612} {:answer/id #uuid "e17522ae-4622-53a9-bb60-7bec4bbcce91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdba31b-0f4f-583e-a52e-f0510ad8e3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77258" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot \\left(i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\left(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0\\right)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492f777b-8264-55ab-9be1-d1359bd9513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a5540-6762-5260-9b7f-2a98061bf95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424872} {:answer/id #uuid "50e4b7f0-64ef-587b-bfde-a4520b836560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424873} {:answer/id #uuid "d60102cb-368c-5209-b3c3-06d9c44b89a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424880} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55c585-a616-50c9-9382-ffb7b5a8ceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55fccd-c2b9-544b-881d-b59a7e7c0f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b70eaac-0c45-5da5-8a2f-2002d9071035" :question/math-academy-id "q-89727" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/3|}}{6},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f33f1e-6e04-5ef0-8a18-a9a9635e495a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424869} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc40166-55c4-53b3-bf06-562cddc8e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0df617-e565-5181-bd27-09321bb7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424877} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9b386-cf97-5734-82c8-f8b9318ea865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424878} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc1dbf-3cbe-5835-99b1-f9050b0ed224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} :answer-field/id #uuid "5340e926-2580-5f2f-bf08-46d25e342346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "97c91681-ff70-5452-bbfe-e1a92bb2e9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126156" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}(x-1)(x-4),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t\\right)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-\\left(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2596bc-8831-5738-bc87-23664928dc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4e^5+1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424983} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0c4dfe-2a2c-535e-956d-62ad8cee8229" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "48a4330e-b61a-5908-b482-77c947469ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350842" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}={x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{4}\\,dx=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{{e}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}{5}-\\frac{{1}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{5}\\right]-\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}}{5}-\\left(\\frac{{e}^{5}}{25}-\\frac{1}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(5{e}^{5}-{e}^{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(4{e}^{5}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9447cf-647a-5fa5-b1b7-8ad767262b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} {:answer/id #uuid "26c878c9-4005-5ffb-b62a-ccf12e7653a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425495} {:answer/id #uuid "84d7e9cc-2b46-5e49-827d-3dc43ec7a3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425499} {:answer/id #uuid "a04fede6-c5e8-5346-92ee-0dfe50e7363c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425500} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e38c85-b6da-52ad-a873-fe8715cae083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be7f666-e571-52b8-923e-291edd79be24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2adf151d-6d7b-5aee-975a-4588dddc072c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109086" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=18,$ calculate $\\text{Var}\\left[\\frac{1}{3}X+1\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $b=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}\\left[\\frac{1}{3}X+1\\right] & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "12ea18be-0a52-5970-8901-dc87b2946b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425555} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5a544a-4601-5833-b1db-6a435d610e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425603} {:answer/id #uuid "e332198f-9804-5c47-a192-c4c61f2b71c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425628} {:answer/id #uuid "fac3f35e-247f-5c4f-a920-7b829128be6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2145c4be-ed47-597a-9e67-aec8406bd115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425563} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2f02c8-06ce-5a98-ab1f-c1b591fc0345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425602} {:answer/id #uuid "bb919cb2-ae68-571a-94da-1addf82f5855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425616} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf95a36-0d03-5ed7-9044-ecf1eec8e640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425563} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4f9f73-a6b2-5aed-a571-16eb62c53ac2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e593a6d-0976-569a-9243-06239dfd7d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{180(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425586} {:answer/id #uuid "a13a3cac-56f6-5282-bb9d-6964f6f1fbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425611} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c66337-e5af-5c6b-9798-890d60d539c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{540(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425619} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf1ce50-61fa-52ff-b523-7c52c07416d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425611} :answer-field/id #uuid "183e6c64-221e-5b50-ba40-54b67f4a4d30" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6c661f-588e-50fc-bfe9-0af2d5d3381b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425550} {:answer/id #uuid "470a61ec-82f5-594e-a092-e7123d4f5fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425580} {:answer/id #uuid "4e721860-34cf-5e70-88ae-eba7fd8d8762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425587} {:answer/id #uuid "fd96d241-b1c2-5857-818a-9f9e50a9546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425641} :answer-field/id #uuid "9935b968-041b-5391-998c-53108d7a9f15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c7a0680-8ba5-57b0-b5fa-884bcd638943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425596} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5a544a-4601-5833-b1db-6a435d610e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425603} {:answer/id #uuid "aa351d07-d824-58b7-91ec-790920890986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425614} {:answer/id #uuid "fac3f35e-247f-5c4f-a920-7b829128be6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425614} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5970db6-cf36-573d-8824-86ad5ee154aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c9cd435-4b0a-516c-bede-18c713c259dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343389" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-5{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}\\right)\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-15t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c65c3f-51c2-5a52-94fb-939ac7507bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425576} {:answer/id #uuid "4c976545-3bb9-5b3a-96f5-f4efde761a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425585} {:answer/id #uuid "a10e5066-a255-5a58-8bdc-c8135fc299f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{si