wer/id #uuid "7a1780b0-73b8-595d-a50c-408c5b940200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425570} :answer-field/id #uuid "6012ae07-2130-5ce1-9441-f9d072891406" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a760df-31d9-540e-8b02-c7498c16b770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425566} {:answer/id #uuid "2e484c03-8f97-5543-a552-957117af437b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425569} {:answer/id #uuid "66ac0b37-b9ac-57b6-8b95-0a58ea6334aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425595} {:answer/id #uuid "9f887b36-0462-5535-bfe6-773f392176f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425607} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d3ab45-43dd-5f98-abc0-1fde975cee55" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8bfe2ba-a961-5858-9456-3382dda97cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343397" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-7{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼\\left[-\\frac{7}{3}t\\right]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234f71f2-30af-5f96-9d21-0a216e6cd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425564} {:answer/id #uuid "33b5f53b-4451-59ac-9316-ec8c82800f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :ans