id #edb/ref 17592186441937} {:answer/id #uuid "dfde5386-3bce-5b33-9c94-b7a8bce6aae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441993} {:answer/id #uuid "e18b5264-28de-5ba9-ad7c-7c951daf466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441993} :answer-field/id #uuid "b671978e-add6-5314-969b-c27ee2b03359" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cce92eb-4d82-5c1d-addd-ef44b4743b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441942} {:answer/id #uuid "5c33773a-b8d7-57a2-b788-d3b5be35adbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441967} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2a9861-240c-5c23-896e-530cd96bb177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441986} {:answer/id #uuid "dcde2d0f-82c7-5c1d-af65-7619a5e452c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441942} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1dea22-bf85-52ce-b7c9-10f7f0673293" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4855477-bdfa-5214-b5d7-5a97d7d6e1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-331077" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}\n$$ given $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nLet $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ={{field-1}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}=L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={A}^{′}(t)\\text{ }dt \\\\ dv={{field-3}} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ {{field-5}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536ba388-fdb8-58ea-b126-38fbd2dcdb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442233} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f93b2c-abf4-5cbd-aa5b-d281bc9dc445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442239} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5cc6be-612b-5620-9ea1-537324d438b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442240} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbd51c-9d36-5509-bb8b-877222cbaef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442242} {:answer/id #uuid "de993e24-618b-5743-81ef-393206c8a109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243} :answer-field/id #uuid "53872032-3b68-54a9-b047-b5722ee1ce8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19d2b758-b269-5c92-aaaf-707449daf876" :question/math-academy-id "q-42301" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9f6016ee4c93aec58f8fff0a8002057f89d7c1bae589e5357a25709ae5a76c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{sin}2t⟩$ for $t\\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f00612-ca24-58a9-9a99-5bc9dff70695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\sin }^{3}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442225} {:answer/id #uuid "244e3377-d03b-59bc-9a15-821691749cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\sin }^{4}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} {:answer/id #uuid "3b19e5e7-17b2-5ecd-8bda-2f5d049ec56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442230} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac2fdfe-d325-50d4-8f82-69274f9797ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\sin }^{3}⁡t{\\cos }^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442231} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93679f-96a9-5546-b831-28f2a59ed0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\sin }^{3}⁡t{\\cos }^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} :answer-field/id #uuid "1007d99f-4a44-5eca-8672-e3953d3b25aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30739161-7e72-5e37-b131-9975b77440f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42315" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfec7cbdb26a3db773ab1f6207de436ff0856b331cda183da530dbb6b91d91bd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t)\\cdot (-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{4}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079819d9-6be2-54b5-a40a-e93e9bbceca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442226} {:answer/id #uuid "30812918-b09d-5eac-98bf-46dd91f25063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442229} {:answer/id #uuid "6baabb12-e16e-5f6d-acae-67851198dc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442234} {:answer/id #uuid "9beda9f3-8b47-5843-a2c9-b0565509b47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238} :answer-field/id #uuid "589fe02c-92ec-567f-8354-84271d506420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55130439-a2e5-560a-bbed-4ce47334253d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126757" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\operatorname{cos}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}4t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14451dd1-51ba-5cb4-a62a-0a5d4ff055bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442227} {:answer/id #uuid "6f80c7b4-33fd-57db-876b-025e20021ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} {:answer/id #uuid "81dd9c9a-88ed-5edc-bb4a-ab112606e4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442236} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4d50f5-796c-5fc2-a622-14130e42fc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442241} {:answer/id #uuid "e911610a-7c47-5303-9138-bd0e5e56ae76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a72b7a-4d2b-5cb1-bfa9-dcf075591732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6653118e-2998-5812-b728-f3217528d405" :question/math-academy-id "q-126746" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65441b2f-7b3a-53c5-af33-45bf0ced0a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310} :answer-field/id #uuid "60abe397-ada8-52f7-89c3-1ccbe2f29023" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e1280de-34a4-5310-b0ea-3ca5ec3ba975" :question/math-academy-id "q-210763" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-|5x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4529e7-604e-506b-adaa-ff85312c20f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-x^{2}+8x-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff772f-b783-5f66-89ac-69df3d1d8f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "990f1bb6-e595-59df-8c02-d323344bc3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-210785" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t\\right)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c66a62f-9a33-540e-bb1b-485e50f03db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442303} {:answer/id #uuid "12f66496-4995-5d37-a001-943fa360d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442305} {:answer/id #uuid "5de59784-191d-59f0-ab4e-cb2aafa6fe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} {:answer/id #uuid "7295486c-b630-5ce0-8356-5ab47719adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442311} {:answer/id #uuid "af97dd8a-6b84-534d-9a5b-b7c3f09bac24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e871fb-e944-567c-b4fe-9835a0a9327f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e65ef0d7-6649-5f56-9e79-7407f7595f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-89621" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64}x(16-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0<x<4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{64}t(16-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{64}\\left(8{t}^{2}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}\\right)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\left(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075a0264-5d31-51b8-ab78-b715207f554f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442333} {:answer/id #uuid "144c1491-1de4-51f2-a930-02e621153cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442341} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9b4db-57aa-527f-982e-08d064fe5e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442346} {:answer/id #uuid "d5564a95-8280-5215-8576-fd338256eb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442393} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ba91a2-8bca-546c-8c29-3bc463185291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442346} :answer-field/id #uuid "156999d6-f605-58e2-adc5-8ad1f3a19157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ffab9e9-5351-5fcd-b618-932668f56d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-43043" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a positive number and a negative number, so the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)=-\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\right) & =-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 9}{3\\cdot 2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{3\\cdot 2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{6}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104d699a-65f0-5f9c-99d0-9f74f993a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442338} {:answer/id #uuid "1fed7d6c-2be1-5cec-bd7e-8f752ef6b918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442347} {:answer/id #uuid "3386c3ad-9434-5e15-919e-f742474174c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442351} {:answer/id #uuid "46c2713e-c655-5a9d-ab4d-07367762c4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442357} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e3ba24-3a51-51d3-b8a4-81746a3ba719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442338} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0e026c-829d-5bf5-9811-b53905fa0394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "319279a3-ba94-5bbe-b88b-0e4da5c9c0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-99503" :question/problem "$4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{16}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a positive number and a negative number, so the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{16}\\right)=-\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\right) & =-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{4\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{4}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{4}\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81324ec4-a2d1-5649-b3ff-74009143c42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442375} :answer-field/id #uuid "0367452d-3fb5-5cfe-b7ee-467659278ae0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c3a1d41-b0b4-56cb-9c22-249c40340608" :question/math-academy-id "q-180905" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\n$${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a negative number and a positive number, so the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{7\\cdot 2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{7\\cdot 2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 1}{7\\cdot \\cancel{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d706c2-3988-50fc-b1c6-a770497d92fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{28}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442348} {:answer/id #uuid "5e95f5b1-b4a5-579f-998b-9d5b23bd331b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{35}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442366} {:answer/id #uuid "7b245d68-aba9-5195-8e36-cc227126fee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442373} {:answer/id #uuid "9b487510-ff34-5b5c-9fa0-b7433775f1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442379} {:answer/id #uuid "db017e05-a943-5ca7-b8b6-a2de5063e487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442373} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d373760-5a89-5b26-bc5e-1afb0e01c86e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c193a4aa-2a3b-50a7-9351-eac6230e1fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42994" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a negative number and a positive number, so the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=-\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\right) & =-\\left(\\frac{7\\cdot 5}{3\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{35}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{35}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c72710f-c2e6-5f98-9934-8c0e10db39f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442441} {:answer/id #uuid "406a5e50-f9e7-5e1a-b74c-548956a80fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442448} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8dbd40-65b4-55c4-825d-2641cb8d37e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442457} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5cce45-dc22-5511-8d13-472d6c0e63cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{2}-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442462} {:answer/id #uuid "e54da0da-f5e4-504a-8ffb-b66a2d635d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442468} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbeb3358-f2ea-5a4a-8dce-c539f418c1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c3b5fdf-e81f-5835-8ccd-060ca0cabad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73556" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3y{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3y{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}3y[{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}3y{\\left[\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}3y\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{4}\\right)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3}{4}y({e}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3}{4}y({e}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}-1)\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}-1)\\left(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "812b461a-180e-543d-b8c3-60aec2c44810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442454} {:answer/id #uuid "99e7e82f-77a2-5e4c-b50f-29924bf0f0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442456} {:answer/id #uuid "9c03be65-b878-58d4-b8c0-c80d0b51e294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442459} {:answer/id #uuid "bece9adc-021f-5779-a2a6-dc4757a9f75c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442465} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b8ce6c-3373-5ebe-bc16-7d2a05cfc3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442465} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c915c1b-8d9a-5d22-96f6-4f765b2be4a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "537e39b9-c554-5e1b-a6dd-8e198218680c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129548" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{y{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{y{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\right]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\right]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f8087da-0be9-5661-8053-ef364f6d08fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442442} {:answer/id #uuid "258eca96-5244-5ca3-b0df-8541f47f4e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442443} {:answer/id #uuid "6d559819-b00a-5b69-960c-d3f8320ab0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442452} {:answer/id #uuid "87b99526-1110-5b37-a9b1-d2cd1aa88253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442455} {:answer/id #uuid "b2329388-28ae-5d8d-9a05-1bffa3cfe3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442442} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b03b87-7f60-5a41-9452-146569702c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a0ea10f-00cc-5f1e-9234-e05424315508" :question/math-academy-id "q-73595" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{y}^{4}}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{y}^{4}}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{2}^{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{y}^{4}}\\left(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{12}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{12}{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\left[-\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{2}^{3}}+\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324de9dd-38ea-5337-bbde-d854373f1914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442445} {:answer/id #uuid "371c3423-1157-51d6-97e6-966f489c5487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442447} {:answer/id #uuid "714a7070-159e-549a-85fe-1667dab928ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442453} {:answer/id #uuid "a986dfcc-797e-520c-8aef-ae24d77f71bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442461} {:answer/id #uuid "c2466043-9164-57ad-b3f9-ec4567efd6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a1bbcd-9f99-5ffd-9c62-c5d07cca7e90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec2c5b36-cc64-59b6-941f-e3ef54e2e8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72136" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $2$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $-1$ to $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\right]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{x-1}{y+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\right]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:answer/id #uuid "3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442572} {:answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442577} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442592} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b10125-03d9-569f-b489-2af1cd51651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442566} {:answer/id #uuid "625e5e39-ae98-52ef-bfcc-de3e0bace052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442579} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442596} {:answer/id #uuid "f33db979-e5fc-564b-9e72-04e2ed505855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442598} {:answer/id #uuid "f911a805-3f13-582e-9b9e-8ec61aec91fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442596} :answer-field/id #uuid "be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127596" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,1,1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{({e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}(-⟨0,1,1⟩+2⟨0,1,0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0(0)+1(1)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442594} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c25002-d570-5188-8443-f9f108b5d95d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532" :question/math-academy-id "q-223120" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨\\,0\\,,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-2|\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b50b45b-e31f-500e-9496-cc48c7886b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442574} {:answer/id #uuid "63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442580} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b0f6c4-1c16-5380-8c5e-e758a32ebbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442587} {:answer/id #uuid "b10046fc-c183-59e3-ae38-7e794395db17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442591} {:answer/id #uuid "f08e5f05-4e3e-5da4-b635-cb93796cbc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442580} :answer-field/id #uuid "9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74850" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩,\\,t\\ge 0 \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1} &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\right)⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩+({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00435226-3a19-5eb9-bdc3-0cc889991ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442563} {:answer/id #uuid "496f3c19-e7a7-5744-b55a-b824329dc81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442573} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442581} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c6b0e9-05cf-5400-9c8e-e91ea5905657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442588} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a90765-83c0-5ed9-bd70-667b33af249d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}⟨-\\sqrt{6},1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442581} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcaf563-74bd-51e5-bbaf-671c2387e005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86bd8ee0-d1c5-5e4f-9534-8a4e93bb860c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127597" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6t}{{(1+3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{6(1)}{{(1+3(1)^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{1+3(1)^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+2⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{4}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(1\\cdot (-3)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{6})+3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(-3-6+9) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09429f0b-5a31-576e-b53c-7a9c9089a259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442565} {:answer/id #uuid "4df4322e-2ca8-59b0-b25f-d74e9d345855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442575} {:answer/id #uuid "512116d3-97dd-54be-bc16-10567d150eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}⟨1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442576} {:answer/id #uuid "54e8dd13-f4d3-59cf-aea8-647ea53c3240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442578} {:answer/id #uuid "9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442586} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e89c597-7b48-520a-9aa5-d249aa23a780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc713dbc-3c1f-5a9c-8a2b-b4377300bc92" :question/math-academy-id "q-75410" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\right)\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(0)\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e39ea1-4cba-57a5-ab90-7a89782cb8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442564} {:answer/id #uuid "34323a95-3364-5d8b-ae6b-9b380e0bf2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442569} {:answer/id #uuid "37ad2308-55f9-592a-86af-f156397fae09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442570} {:answer/id #uuid "77fd4f80-1bca-57c5-b06d-9f6931a1ae23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442582} {:answer/id #uuid "b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442593} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8a9865-39bf-545e-aeef-35a531125f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f08885d9-557e-5e55-b967-a7cb452a9e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127379" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P\\left(2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\left\\langle 2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle ,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P\\left(2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\left\\langle 2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ \\left\\langle 2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle  & =\\left\\langle 2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=2 \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P\\left(2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right)⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨2,\\,t⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}(-t⟨2,t⟩+({t}^{2}+4)⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{((1)^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}⟨-2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2(-1)+1(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442583} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f357a402-4ed9-5871-9002-ee2ada823334" :question/math-academy-id "q-358780" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\left(-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12)\\,k\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{5}{13}‖\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{5}{13}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d20667f-50b2-55b0-95fa-20f52b560b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442747} {:answer/id #uuid "9d738677-4b48-5272-ba62-6f070fe8babb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442751} {:answer/id #uuid "a8188bf8-8b54-575b-933a-aa859ce24d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442752} {:answer/id #uuid "d5079a63-2c21-5532-8a24-134a5b4af955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442753} {:answer/id #uuid "de7d22b9-d57c-5d7b-88cb-f0b4ca5f4a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442755} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd74140-69a0-5af3-8672-f40c3db56533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6caf644a-b965-5b35-b048-eee4060c43c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161034" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3bef2dbb84cfe3a2881abf8f61761f98ea0d9cf4a07ea582355c57d375f975d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $3\\le u\\le 6.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right),\\,3\\le u\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b976e62862ba82dfd89ff28627239dd5b5d1be1144e257223c091f29a5729f89.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=3,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $3.$\n\n- When $u=6,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $6.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:answer/id #uuid "03d35f72-203f-50ad-acf8-60daf8ec86f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=\\left\\langle \\frac{3s}{10},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443490} {:answer/id #uuid "18b22410-7856-5e1a-acc1-6826159d44dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=\\left\\langle 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{4}\\right),\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{4}\\right)\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443491} {:answer/id #uuid "1efba70b-6e3e-5b18-88b3-cce733abfbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=\\left\\langle 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443492} {:answer/id #uuid "21552ed1-365f-5f5f-9f16-05c0709bc3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\left[{\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)}^{3/4}-1\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443493} {:answer/id #uuid "3329a212-2843-5cea-8b79-3333d2a3bf2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\left[{\\left(\\frac{s}{4}-1\\right)}^{2/3}+1\\right]}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443495} {:answer/id #uuid "447ff6ac-8e33-53a7-a4b3-bb9df76bffb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{35}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{7}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{14},\\,-\\frac{1}{35}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{7}\\right)\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443498} {:answer/id #uuid "63ae5483-af4f-5b7e-81e0-1879fd88e830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[{\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}+1\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443500} {:answer/id #uuid "6486f987-8f88-59d1-a346-9c83d923a1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{7}\\right),\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{7}\\right)\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443501} {:answer/id #uuid "88524411-7322-5c13-a0d6-4635b37af634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 